window.IX_GIT206_REF = {"Renal blood flow autoregulation":[{"src":"Physiology Q&A (renal)","head":"1. Discuss Regulation of the renal blood flow ?","text":"1. Autoregulation of RBF & GFR: in denervated & isolated kidney\n  •  RBF & GFR →kept constant despite changes in ABP (90 – 200 mmHg)\n  A- Myogenic autoregulation:\n  ➢  ↑ABP → stretch of afferent arteriole→ open Ca++ channels→ VC → prevent increase in RBF\n  B- Tubulo-glomerular balance (feedback)\n  ↑ ABP→↑RBF & GFR  ↓ABP→↓ RBF & GFR\n  → ↑Solutes delivery at macula dense  ↓ Solutes delivery at macula dense\n  → ↑production of adenosine  → ↑production of renin (JGC)→ ↑ Ag II→\n  → VC of afferent arterioles  → VC of efferent arteriole\n  •  Aim: maintain constant GFR despite marked changes in ABP\n 2. Nervous regulation (sympathetic) of renal blood flow (α receptor)→ VC →↓ RBF & GFR"}],"GFR and its regulation":[{"src":"Physiology Q&A (renal)","head":"3. Explain the factors affecting GFR or………………………..effect of………….. on GFR?","text":"1. Filtration coefficient (Kf):\n  o ↑KF→ ↑GFR\n  o Depends on Permeability & Surface area\n  • Permeability: ↑thickness of glomerular membrane (as in uncontrolled DM & HTN)→↓permeability\n  • Surface area: affected by\n  A- Contraction of mesangial cells  B- Relaxation of mesangial cells\n  e.g. by Vasopressin, NE  e.g. by ANP, PGE2,\n  ↓surface area→↓ GFR  ↑surface area→↑ GFR\n  chronic uncontrolled DM→ also ↓ number of functional capillaries→ ↓surface area\n  2. Glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure ↑ HPGC→ ↑ GFR:\n  Diameter of afferent arteriole  Diameter of efferent arteriole  ABP\n  • VD: PGE2 → ↑HP GC →↑ GFR  ‫القانون‬\n  • VC: NE → ↓ HPGC → ↓ GFR  • Moderate VC (Ang II) →↑ HPGC\n  ↑ Sympathetic →↓GFR < 50%  → ↑GFR.\n  Mechanism of Auto-regulation\n  3. Bowman’s capsule hydrostatic pressure stone in ureter→↓GFR\n2. 4. Glomerular colloid osmotic pressure\n  ↑ GC e.g., dehydration → ↓ GFR  ↓ GC e.g., hypoproteinemia→↑ GFR.\n  5. Renal Vasodilators:\n  o PGE2, PGl2 → cause VD →↑ RBF and GFR.\n  o Anti-inflammatory drug →↓PG →↓GFR\n  o Sympathetic & angiotensin II→↑PG → protect kidney from severe VC as in hemorrhage.\n  6. Autonomic nerves (sympathetic) (α receptor)→ cause VC →↓ RBF & GFR\n  7. Effect of protein intake ↑RBF and GFR.\n  o ↑protein intake →↑amino acids filter in Bowman's capsule→↑ amino acids and Na+ reabsorption in PCT\n  →↓ Na delivery at macula dense→ tubule-glomerular feedback → cause VC of efferent arteriole"},{"src":"Physiology Q&A (renal)","head":"2. Define GFR, mention the forces responsible for its control?","text":"Definition Volume of filtrate formed by glomeruli of both kidneys/minute\nNormal GFR 125 ml/min in men.  10% less in women.\n ➢ 125 ml/min = 7.5 L/h =180 L/day.  normal urine volume is 1 L/day,  ≥ 99% is reabsorbed\n  ➢ Forces responsible for its control:\n  Starling equation: GFR = KF (HPGC - HPBC) - (GC - BC)\n  1. Hydrostatic pressure gradient across capillary wall\n  2. Osmotic pressure gradient across capillary wall\n  3. KF = glomerular ultrafiltration co-efficient (ml/min/mmHg), depends on Permeability & surface area\n  •  GFR = KF X NFP\n  Forces favoring filtration (mmHg):  Forces opposing filtration\n  HPGC =60 mmHg  HPBC = 18 mmHg\n  BC= 0 mmHg (no protein is filtered)  GC = 32 mmHg.\n  Net filtering pressure = 60 - 18 - 32 = 10 mmHg."}],"Sodium excretion and its hormonal control":[{"src":"Physiology Q&A (renal)","head":"6. Discuss the factors affecting the Regulation of Na+ Excretion?","text":"1. GFR: \"Glomerulo-tubular Balance\" ↑GFR → ↑ Na+ reabsorption\nMechanism in isolated kidney ,  Renal tubules reabsorb constant % of filtered Na+ (65%)\nSite PCT, Loop of Henle\nImportance Prevent overloading of DCT/  Prevent inappropriate losses of Na+ and water\n2. Rate of Tubular Flow Slow flow rate →↑ Na+ reabsorption\n3. Effect of ABP \"Pressure Natriuresis & Diuresis \" ↑ABP →↑ Na+ excretion\nMechanism compensatory mechanism for regulation of ABP independent of nervous or hormone\n  1. ↑ABP →↓Ang II secretion\n  2. ↑ ABP →↑hydrostatic pressure in peritubular capillaries →enhance back leak of Na+ into lumen\n\f4. Hormonal Control:\n Hormones →↑Na+ reabsorption\n Aldosterone (mineralocorticoid)  Acts on P cells on DCT & CD\n  o At basolateral membrane: ↑Na-K ATPase\n  o In luminal membrane: ↑ Na+ channel\n Glucocorticoid  weak mineralocorticoid effect\n Sex Hormones (estrogen)\n Angiotensin II:  Stimulates aldosterone secretion\n  Direct action on PCT: Stimulates Na+ - K+ ATPase pump.\n Most powerful\n  Stimulates Na+ - H+ counter transport.\n sodium-retaining hormone\n  VC of efferent arterioles→ ↓hydrostatic pressure, ↑osmotic pressure\n  of peritubular capillaries\n Hormones →↓Na+ reabsorption\n Atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP)  Mechanism:\n in case of ↑ECF  ✓ ↑ GFR → ↑filtered Na+ →↑Na+ excretion via\n  • Relaxation of mesangial cells→↑ surface area\n  • VD of afferent\n  ✓ Inhibit renin secretion →↓angiotensin-II & aldosterone.\n  ✓ On collecting ducts: Inhibit Na+ channels & Na+ - K+ ATPase\n PGE2  ↑Na+ excretion through:\n  o At basolateral membrane: Inhibit Na+ - K+ ATPase.\n  o At apical membrane: Inhibit Na+ channels.\n Endothelin  ↑PGE2.\n5. Sympathetic stimulation: ↑Na+ reabsorption &↓Na+ excretion by\n  •  VC of renal vessels (via 1 receptor) →↓RBF and GFR\n  •  ↑renin & angiotensin II\n  •  "},{"src":"Physiology Q&A (renal)","head":"5. Describe Na+ Reabsorption in different segments of Renal Tubule? Handling","text":"Na+ is reabsorbed at all segments except descending segment of loop of Henle\n 1. Proximal Tubule reabsorb 65%\n Mechanism of Na reabsorption Primary active transport\n  ➢ At basolateral border: Na+ K+ ATPase pump → ↓intracellular Na+ & produce negative potential\n  ➢ At luminal border: Na+ diffuses from lumen into cell\n First half= early PCT  Late half\n Na+ reabsorbed by  Na+ is reabsorbed with\n • Co-transport with (sulphate, PO4) & all filtered glucose, amino acids  passive diffusion of Cl-\n • Counter-transport with H+ secretion and reabsorption of HCO3 (85 %)"},{"src":"Physiology Q&A (renal)","head":"➢ At basolateral membrane: Na+ - K+ ATPase?","text":"➢ In luminal membrane: Na+ and K channels\n  o Na diffuse into P-cells , K diffuse out in lumen\n  o passive reabsorption of Cl\n  Aldosterone inhibitor= Aldactone: inhibit Na- K exchange at DCT & CD→ ↑Na excretion & K retention"}],"Tubular reabsorption (glucose, clearance)":[{"src":"Physiology Q&A (renal)","head":"9. Describe the mechanism of Glucose reabsorption by renal tubules? define……..","text":"Site all filtered glucose is reabsorbed in early proximal tubule\n  Mechanism: 2nd active transport\n  At luminal brush border 2nd active transport\n  • Glucose & Na+ bind SGLT-2\n  ➢ As Na+ diffuse along its electrochemical gradient\n  ➢ glucose is carried into the cells against concentration gradient\n  At basolateral border Glucose is carried into ISF by GlUT-2 (facilitated diffusion)\n  Transport Maximum of glucose (TmG) maximum amount of glucose (in mg) reabsorbed / minute.\n  • Indicator of reabsorptive capacity of the kidney , Determined by number of glucose carriers in PCT\n  Value 300 mg / min in female 375 mg /min in male\n  Renal threshold for glucose plasma glucose level at which glucose appears in urine\n  Value Arterial blood: 200 mg / dl Venous blood: 180 mg"},{"src":"Final 197 GIT, solved","head":"3-Explain the site and mechanism of glucose","text":"reabsorption by nephrons of kidney\nSite| all filtered glucose is reabsorbed in early proximal tubule Tubular lumen Intorstitium\nMechanism; 2\" active transport 7\nNat\nAt luminal brush borderPAeladcmerlaseelae a\nGlucose\ne Glucose & Na* bind SGLT-2\n> As Na* diffuse along its electrochemical gradient\n> glucose is carried into the cells against concentration gradient\nVaR ELelmelecesag Glucose is carried into ISF by GIUT-2 (facilitated diffusion)\nPel Lea MEL meleleret-e@enc}] Maximum amount of glucose (in mg) reabsorbed / minute.\ne Indicator of reabsorptive capacity of the kidney , Determined by number of glucose carriers in PCT\n300 mg / mininfemale 375 mg /min in male\n*\narm dalesulel(emielm-leaex) plasma glucose level at which glucose appears in urine he\nArterial blood: 200 mg / dl Venous blood: 180 mg\nAnatomy :\n4- Discribe the attachment and 3 important features of\nexternal oblique muscle\nLower & ribs\nHE OVvigin :\nX in Sevtion : Pleshy: Ant Vy ef ovterliP of iliac crest.\nAP, nemresis. Attached to: Linea alba, symPhyis PubiS /Xi Phoid ProZesS\ne ASIS\nx3 imrtant features : Lower tree borer ! fololed uPon it Self —-» Favminy inuinal lige ment athached te) oe Luberc le\nforms : SuPerficial inquinal ring\nAPneuvesis Gutinue as external SPrmetic PaSCia of SPermatic Grd."},{"src":"Physiology Q&A (renal)","head":"12.Describe the mechanism of H2O reabsorption by renal tubule?","text":"➢ Passive process throughout the whole nephron except ascending loop of Henle  2 types\n  A- Obligatory water reabsorption 87% of filtered water reabsorbed by osmosis independent of ADH\n 1- Proximal tubule (65%) 2- Loop of Henle: (15%)  3- Early Distal tubule\n  Main site  Descending limb  Ascending limb\n Solute permeability\n Reabsorb 65%  No solutes reabsorption  reabsorb solutes  Reabsorb Solutes\n H2O permeability\n Highly permeable  Highly permeable  Impermeable to H2O  Relatively impermeable\n Reabsorb 65% of H2O  Reabsorb 15% of H2O into  to H2O\n via aquaporin-1 channel  hypertonic medullary ISF\n Osmolarity  of  tubular\n fluid\n iso-osmotic with Plasma  =1200 -1400 at end of diluted  diluted\n =300 m Osmo/L  descending limb  (100 mOsm/L),  (60 mOsm/L.).\n  Diluting segment: thick ascending limb of loop of Henle and early distal tubule\n4- late DCT, collecting duct: water reabsorption through these segments\n  Facultative water reabsorption  Obligatory water reabsorption\n  13 %  7%\n  Controlled by ADH  Independent of ADH\n  •  Role of ADH→ ↑ H2O permeability via insertion of aquaporin-2 channel in luminal membrane of principal\n  cells of late DCT, cortical, medullary CD\n\f  Requirements for excreting concentrated urine"}],"Urine concentration (loop of Henle, countercurrent, ADH)":[{"src":"Physiology Q&A (renal)","head":"13.Define Countercurrent system, explain its role in urine concentration?","text":"1. Countercurrent multiplier system inflow runs parallel to, counter to , in close proximity to outflow\nFunction of loop of Henle of juxtamedullary nephron\n • Adds solutes to medullary ISF→ creates medullary hyperosmolarity\n Thick Ascending limb  Thin Ascending limb  Descending limb\n Solute permeability\n Co transport of Na+, K+ and Cl  Passive reabsorption of Na+ & Cl less Impermeable to Na+, Cl\n Water permeability\n Impermeable to water  Impermeable to water  Permeable to water\n Tubular fluid osmolarity\n ↓osmolarity (100 mosm/L)  ↓osmolarity  ↑ Osmolarity from 300 to 1200 at\n  tip of the loop"},{"src":"Physiology Q&A (renal)","head":"1. High ADH: (role of ADH)?","text":"2. Hyperosmotic renal medulla ISF (4): from 300 to 1200 mOsm/L\nMechanisms produce hyperosmotic renal medullary ISF"}],"Renal acid-base: H+ secretion and buffering":[{"src":"Physiology Q&A (renal)","head":"Explain the Fate of H+ secreted by renal tubule? Importance of H+ buffering?","text":"1. In PCT by HCO3 buffer\n2. In Distal tubule and collecting duct\nA- Buffering by phosphate buffer\n  a) H+ + Na2HP04→ NaH2PO4 + Na+\n  b) NaH2PO4 is excreted → titratable acidity in urine\n  c) Na+ is reabsorbed with intracellular HCO3-\nB- Buffering by ammonia (NH3)\n  a. NH3 is formed from glutamine\n  Glutaminase\n  Glutamine ⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯→Glutamic acid + NH3\n  b. NH3 + H+ →NH4\n  c. NH4+ is excreted in urine\n  d. Na+ is reabsorbed with intracellular HCO3-\nImportance of H+ buffering\n  o H+ secretion in DCT and collecting ducts as long as\n  ▪  pH of the fluid in these segments > 4.5 (limiting pH for H+ secretion).\n  o If secreted H+ is not buffered→ this pH is reached rapidly → cause stop of H+ secretion."}],"Gastric acid secretion and its control":[{"src":"Physiology Q&A (GIT)","head":"7. List Stimuli of HCl secretion & their mechanism of action, functions of HCL?                    ‫نظري وعمل‬","text":"Functions of HCL\n  1. Activate pepsinogen → pepsin.\n  2. Provide optimum pH for action of pepsin.\n  3. Dissolves food\n  4. Helps iron & calcium absorption\n  5. Sterility of stomach\n  6. Stimulates flow of bile.\nStimuli of HCl secretion & their mechanism of action\n  Stimuli  Receptor  2nd messenger\n  Histamine  H2 receptors  ↑ cAMP\n  Acetylcholine  M3 muscarinic receptors  ↑ Ca++.\n  Gastrin  Directly by ↑ Ca++ or\n  Indirectly through ↑secretion of histamine from (ECL cells)\n  •  2nd messengers → ↑number of pump proteins in the cell membrane"},{"src":"Physiology Q&A (GIT)","head":"8. Describe 3 Phases of Gastric Secretion?             ‫نظري وعمل‬","text":"1. Cephalic stimulatory phase: (Nervous)→ 1/3 of gastric secretion\n  •  Both conditioned reflex and unconditioned reflexes → stimulate vagus →↑ gastric secretion by:\n  a. Acetylcholine → acts directly on gastric glands in the body & fundus\n  b. Gastrin-releasing peptide →↑ gastrin secretion.\n2. Gastric stimulatory phase: (Nervous and Hormonal) 2/3 gastric secretion\n  a. Long vago-vagal reflexes\n  b. Local enteric reflexes\n  c. Gastrin secretion.\n3. Intestinal inhibitory phase: (Nervous and Hormonal)→↓ gastric secretion by:\n  A. Enterogastric reflex.\n  B.  Hormones inhibit gastric secretion, such as GIP, VIP and secretin."},{"src":"Physiology Q&A (renal)","head":"Enumerate Factors affecting acid secretion?","text":"1. Aldosterone: ↑ H+ secretion\n  2. Intracellular PCO2: ↑PCO2 (in respiratory acidosis)→↑ H+ secretion.\n  3. ECF K+ concentration: in hyperkalemia →↓H+ secretion\n  4. Carbonic anhydrase inhibitor: ↓hydrogen secretion\nAcid base  Respiratory acidosis  Respiratory alkalosis Metabolic acidosis  Metabolic alkalosis\nDisturbance  (↓pH, ↑CO2, H+)  (↑pH, ↓CO2, H+)  (↓pH, ↓HCO3, ↑H+,) (↑pH, ↑HCO3, ↓H+)\nCauses  Hypoventilation  Primary  1. ↑production  of 1.Persistent vomiting\n  •  R.C. depression  hyperventilation  fixed acids  HCl- are lost in vomitus\n  Narcotics  •  High altitudes  •  DKA  & ↑HCO3- in the\n  sedation  •  Exercise  •  Shock  plasma.\n  •  Airway obstruction: •  Anxiety  •  Aspirin poisoning\n  Bronchial asthma  2.↑intake of alkali\n  •  Fevers  2. ↓ elimination of\n  •  Paralysis  of  to treat peptic ulcer\n  respiratory muscles  fixed acid: Renal failure\n  3. Cushing syndrome\n  3. Loss of HCO3-\n  4. Conn's syndrome.\n  •  Prolonged  or\n  Severe diarrhea.\n  •  Addison's disease\nCompensation  Renal  Renal  Renal  Renal\n  • ↑H+ secretion+  • ↓ H+ secretion+ • ↑ HCO3 reabsorption • ↓ HCO3- reabsorption +\n  ↑HCO3- reabsorption ↓HCO3- reabsorption↑H+ secretion  ↓ H+ secretion\n  Respiratory  Respiratory\n  • ↑H+ →↑ ventilation  • ↓ [H+] → ↓ventilation\n  →↓ PCO2, H+  →↑ PCO2\n  To norma\n\fSite of H secretion\n• H+ is secreted in all renal tubule except descending and ascending thin limbs of the loop of Henle\n  For each H+ secreted, one bicarbonate is reabsorbed.\n• Bicarbonate reabsorption\n  o Site\n  PCT  85%\n  Thick ascending loop of Henle  10%\n  Collecting duct  4.8%\nAbsorption by peritubular capillaries by bulk flow as venous end of capillary\n Forces favor reabsorption  Forces oppose reabsorption\n Hydrostatic pressure in renal ISF (6mmHg)  Hydrostatic pressure inside peritubula"}],"GIT motility (BER, peristalsis, vomiting, defecation)":[{"src":"Physiology Q&A (GIT)","head":"1. Describe slow waves: (Basic Electric Rhythm) = (BER) of GIT smooth muscle? Activity?","text":"Definition\n  o Slow changes in RMP → In the form of depolarization & repolarization\n  Frequency  varies and determines the frequency of contractions\n  4/min  in stomach\n  12  in duodenum\nCause Initiated by interstitial cells of Cajal\nImportance:\n  • Not causes muscle contraction\n  • Allows smooth muscle to generate spike potentials\n interstitial cells of Cajal (ICCs)\n connected to smooth muscle\n Site  In stomach & small intestine: in circular muscle\n  In colon: at submucosal border\n Function Excitable pacemaker cells → generate slow waves in smooth muscle"},{"src":"Physiology Q&A (GIT)","head":"16.Explain Receptive relaxation (storage) of the stomach?                Motility ?????","text":"Function of proximal motor unit (fundus & body).\n  ➢ Volume of empty stomach: 50 ml\n  ➢ Pressure in the proximal unit = intra-abdominal pressure\n  •  Stimulus: distension\n  •  Control (Regulation):\n  1) Vago-vagal reflex (abolished by vagotomy)\n  2) peristaltic wave during swallowing\n  •  Response: relaxation\n  •  Aim: ↑ capacity"}],"Salivary secretion":[{"src":"Physiology Q&A (GIT)","head":"5. Explain Control of Salivary secretion? = Innervation of salivary glands","text":"Parasympathetic efferent fibers  1. Sympathetic efferent\n Origin  Sublingual and Submandibular Parotid gland\n  salivary glands\n  Superior salivary nucleus in Inferior salivary nucleus in LHCs of 1st & 2nd thoracic\n  medulla  medulla  segments of the spinal cord\n  Chorda tympani branch of Glossopharyngeal nerve  Post ganglionic fibers from\n  facial nerve→  relay in otic ganglion.  cervical ganglion\n  relay  in  submandibular\n  ganglion.\n Actions  •  Marked VD due to local release of VIP  V.C.\n  Profuse watery secretion  Secretion of small amount of\n  •  Atropine→↓ salivary secretion  saliva +\nSecretion of saliva in response to\n  Unconditioned reflexes  Conditioned reflexes\n Stimulus  Taste and thermal stimuli from mouth  Sight, smell\n  or reflexes from stomach and upper intestine  Hearing, preparation of food\n Center  superior and inferior salivary nuclei  impulses from cortex or\n  hypothalamus or amygdala→ excites\n  salivary nuclei\n Response  ↑ salivary secretion\n6. Mechanism of acid secretion‫نظري وعمل‬\n  • HCL is secreted by parietal cell via active process\n  1. H2O dissociates into H+ and OH- in the cell.\n  2. CO2 + H2O carbonic anhydrase H2CO3  H+ + HCO3-\n  3. HCO3- diffuses to blood in exchange for Cl-\n  4. Cl- is actively transported to the lumen =→ creates -ve potential\n  5. Leads to passive diffusion of K + to lumen.\n  6. H+ is actively secreted to lumen in exchange for K + by H+/K+ ATPase pump (proton pump).\n  7. H2O passes to the lumen by osmosis.\n  Alkaline tide: as HCl is secreted by the parietal cells\n  HCO3- is added to gastric venous blood →↑PH of the blood"},{"src":"Physiology Q&A (GIT)","head":"4. Describe the Mechanism of salivary secretion?","text":"First stage  Second stage\n  Acini secrete 1ry secretion  Ducts modify primary secretion\n  Ionic composition as plasma (ECF)  •  Na+ are actively reabsorbed in exchange with\n  K+ (stimulated by aldosterone)\n  → Cl- is passively reabsorbed\n  •  Net at rest: ↓ Na, Cl in saliva, ↑K > plasma.\n  •  Bicarbonate is actively secreted.\n  •  Impermeable to water.\n  1ry secretion is Isotonic  2nd secretion is hypotonic\n  •  During maximal salivation (parasympathetic stimulation):\n  o Na+, CI- and HCO3- in saliva nearly as in plasma\n  o K+ conc. not ↑, as ductal modification is markedly ↓by rapid flow"}],"Pancreatic secretion and pancreatitis":[{"src":"Physiology Q&A (GIT)","head":"12.Discuss Regulation of Pancreatic Secretion?","text":"1- Nervous regulation\n  o During cephalic & gastric phases of gastric secretion\n  →↑ parasympathetic discharge → release acetylcholine\n  → secretion of pancreatic enzymes.\n2- Hormonal regulation (main mechanism)\n Secretin  Cholecystokinin (CCK):\n Stimulus: acid chyme,  Stimulus: polypeptides, amino acids and fats\n Site of release: upper small intestine  Site of release: upper small intestine\n Action: acts on pancreatic ducts  Action: acting on acinar cells\n → secretion of alkaline pancreatic juice  → secretion of pancreatic enzymes.\n Aim: neutralizes acid chyme & provides appropriate pH\n for action of enzymes."}],"Swallowing (deglutition)":[{"src":"Physiology Q&A (GIT)","head":"Enumerate phases of swallowing & Describe Pharyngeal Stage of Swallowing? ‫نظري وعمل‬","text":"A. Voluntary oral Stage of Swallowing Pressure of the tongue upward and backward against hard palate\n  Helped by contraction of mylohyoid muscle\n  B. Pharyngeal Stage\n  Movement of food through pharynx into esophagus 1-2 seconds\n  Stimulus  bolus of food in pharynx\n  Receptor  around pharyngeal opening\n  Center  in the medulla\n  Result  Rapid peristaltic wave in superior → then middle → then inferior pharyngeal muscles\n  Upper pharyngoesophageal sphincter relaxes\n  Result  movement of food through pharynx into esophagus\n  Protection of air passages during swallowing:\n  Closure of posterior nares  via elevation of soft palate\n  Closure of glottis  Via approximation of vocal cords\n  Elevation of larynx  To be covered by epiglottis\n  Inhibition of respiration  (swallowing apnea) for 1-2 sec\n  C. Involuntary Esophageal Stage\n  1. Primary peristaltic wave: continuation of peristaltic wave, begins in pharynx, 8 to 10 seconds.\n  2. 2nd peristaltic waves: result from distention of the esophagus if primary wave fails to move food"}],"Metabolism (RQ, BMR)":[{"src":"MHR written revision 2024","head":"Discuss importance of Respiratory Quotient (RQ) ?","text":"Definition ratio of CO2 produced to O2 used\nImportance Determination of\n  1.The type of the Oxidized food  RQ for  CHO = 1.0\n  Fats = 0.7\n  Proteins = 0.82\n  Mixed diet = 0.85\n  2. Source of energy used by organ: E.g. In brain, RQ = 0.97 → 0.99 (glucose is main source)\n  3. Pathological disorders\n  In diabetes mellitus  When insulin is given\n  RQ =0.7 → fat is main source  RQ increased to 1.0 due to glucose oxidation\n  4. Energy equivalent of oxygen\n  RQ is 1.0 → CHO  energy equivalent of oxygen = 5 Calories\n  RQ is 0.7 → fats  energy equivalent of oxygen = 4.7 Calories\n  5.Measured for specific organs and body: measuring organ O2 consumption and CO2 / unit time\n  × arterio-venous difference of O2 and CO2 across the organ."},{"src":"MHR written revision 2024","head":"Differences between respiratory quotient and respiratory exchange ratio?","text":"Respiratory exchange ratio (R): ratio of CO2 produced to O2 used by lungs / time\n  • R: at rest < one. = 4 / 5 = 0.8\n  RQ  R\n  Affected only by metabolism Affected by additional factors other than (metabolism)\n  Between 0.7 and 1  Can reach as low as 0.5\n  Can be higher > 1.0 in:\n  1. Hyperventilation → ↑ CO2expired\n  2. Metabolic acidosis: → ↑CO2 expired\n  3. During exercise: ↑ CO2 expired\n  (Hyperventilation + Metabolic acidosis)"}],"Food intake and obesity":[{"src":"MHR written revision 2024","head":"Discuss neural centers controlling food intake?","text":"Centers ↑ food intake  Centers ↓ food intake\n Feeding center  Satiety center\n  ✓ Site: lateral nuclei  ✓ Site: ventromedial nuclei\n  ✓ Stimulation →↑food intake  ✓ Stimulation →↓food intake (vice versa)\n Arcuate nuclei Contain 2 types of neurons\n NPY – AGRP neurons  Pro-opiomelanocortin neurons (POMC)\n → ↑food intake  → ↓ food intake as\n - neuropeptide Y (NPY)  - Cocaine- and amphetamine- related transcript (CART)\n - AGRP (agouti-related protein)  - α-Melanocyte-stimulating hormone\n  - Corticotropin- releasing hormone (CRH)"},{"src":"MHR written revision 2024","head":"Discuss short / Intermediate and long- term regulation of food intake?","text":"A- Short- term regulation to prevent overeating\n1- Oral factors chewing, salivation\n2- Gastrointestinal distension → send inhibitory signals via vagus nerve\n3- Gut Theory:\n  I.  GIT hormones →inhibit feeding center by activating POMC in hypothalamus as:\n  -  CCK → secreted from the intestine\n  -  Peptide YY : secreted from ileum → peak value 2 hours after meals (↑ with fat meal )\n  -  Glucagon-like peptide secreted from intestine →↑ insulin → ↓ food intake\n  II.  GIT hormones →↑ feeding: as ghrelin secreted from oxyntic cells (mainly)\nB) Intermediate and long- term regulation:\n1. Glucostatic theory ↑blood glucose → ↑ Glucose utilization → stimulate satiety center\n2. Lipostatic theoryb leptin secreted by adipocytes → Stimulate leptin receptors\n  1. ↓ NPY and AGRP (stimulator)\n  2. Activates POMC neurons (inhibitor)\n  3. ↑sympathetic activity →↑ metabolic rate. (Noradrenaline binds β3-adrenergic\n  receptors on brown fat→ stimulate uncoupling→↑ heat energy and little ATP.\n3.Thermostatic theory Exposure to cold weather →↑food intake to\n  •  ↑metabolic rate →↑ heat release  ↑ fat stores for insulation"}],"Temperature regulation and fever":[{"src":"MHR written revision 2024","head":"Describe pathophysiology & characteristic of fever (Febrile conditions), mention other cause of fever?","text":"Pyrogens (endotoxins): breakdown products released from bacterial cellmembranes\n  → phagocytosed by leucocytes\n  → release interleukin-1\n  → ↑formation of prostaglandins E2\n  → ↑set-point of hypothalamus\n  → stimulates anti- drop mechanisms → ↑body temperature.\n\f  Antipyretics (aspirin): blocked prostaglandins synthesis from arachidonic acid\n  Characteristics:\n  Chills: 1st few hours  Crisis (Flush): with removal of the cause\n  body temperature < ↑set-point  ↓set-point to normal\n  → shivering  → skin VD & sweating\nFever caused by brain lesions as tumors or surgery→ cause prolonged high temperature"},{"src":"MHR written revision 2024","head":"Discuss Cause , Symptoms and management of heat stroke","text":"Cause: when air is 100% humidified →body temperature ↑as environmental temperature ↑ > 94° F.\n  When body temperature > 105-108°F → cause heatstroke.\n  Symptoms:\n  1. Loss of excessive fluids → shock.\n  2. Abdominal distress + vomiting.\n  3. loss of consciousness\n  exposure to prolonged hyperpyrexia:\n  1. Local hemorrhages and irreversible degeneration of all body cells\n  2. Damage of vital organs & death.\n  Management: Sponge or spray cooling of the skin + Cold water bath (better to be avoided)"}],"Rectus sheath":[{"src":"Jalal written revision (anatomy)","head":"Q.Rectus sheath","text":"*Def.: Aponeurotic sheath enveloping rectus abdominis & pyramidalis.\n  *Formation:\n  Between costal margin &\n  Above costal  Below level midway between\n  level midway between\n  margin  umbilicus & symphysis pubis\n  umbilicus& symphysis pubis\n  -Ant. wall is formed  -Ant. wall is formed of external  -Ant. wall is formed by 3 muscles\n  by external oblique  oblique aponeurosis & ant.  aponeuroses (external abdominal\n  aponeurosis.  lamina of internal abdominal  oblique, internal abdominal oblique\n  oblique aponeurosis.  & transversus\n  -Post. wall is deficient  abdominis).\n  (muscle rests on 5th, 6th &  -Post. wall is formed by post.\n  7th costal cartilages).  lamina of internal abdominal  -Post. wall is deficient & muscle is\n  oblique & transversus abdominis  directly related to fascia\n  aponeuroses.  transversalis.\n*Contents: a)2 muscles: Rectus abdominis & pyramidalis.\nb)2 vessels: Sup. epigastric & inf. epigastric vessels.\nc)Lower 5 intercostal & subcostal nerves & vessels.\nd)Connective tissue, lymphatics (from breast to umbilicus) & sympathetic fibers."}],"Inguinal canal (boundaries, rings, contents, hernia)":[{"src":"Jalal written revision (anatomy)","head":"Q.Inguinal canal","text":"*Def.: Oblique passage through lower part of ant. abdominal wall muscles.\n*Site, length & direction: Above inguinal ligament med. half, 4 cm long, extends downwards,\nforwards & med. between superficial & deep inguinal rings.\n*Beginning, end & boundaries:\n  Beginning  End\n  Boundaries\n  (deep inguinal ring)  (superficial inguinal ring\n-Oval opening in fascia  -Triangular opening in external  -Ant. wall: *Along whole length;\ntransversalis, 1/2 inch  oblique aponeurosis, above &  external abdominal oblique aponeurosis.\nabove mid-inguinal point.  med. to pubic tubercle.  *Lat. 1/2; Lower fleshy fibers of internal\n  abdominal oblique.\n  -Has med. & lat. crura. Med. crus  -Post. wall: *Along whole length;\n  attached to symphysis pubis & lat.  fascia transversalis.\n  crus attached to pubic tubercle. It's  *Med. 1/2; Conjoint tendon.\n-From it's margins, extends  base is at pubic crest.  *Med. 1/4; Inguinal ligament reflected\ninternal spermatic fascia.  part.\n  -From it's margins, extends external  -Roof: Arching lower fleshy fibers of\n  spermatic fascia  internal abdominal oblique & transversus\n-Wider in males, allowing  abdominis.\npassage of spermatic cord  -Wider in males, allowing passage  -Floor: *Along whole length; upper\nin male or round ligament  of spermatic cord & ilio-inguinal  concave surface of inguinal ligament.\nof uterus in females.  n. in males or round ligament of  *Med.; Lacunar ligament.\n  uterus & ilioinguinal n. in females.\n\fModule 206  DIG\n  Rectus sheath\n  lnguinal canal\n\f  Module 206  DIG\n*Contents: a)Spermatic cord & its coverings (internal spermatic fascia & cremasteric muscle\n& fascia) in male or round ligament of uterus in female.\n  b)Ilio-inguinal n.\n  Testis\n*Def. & function: Primary male sex organ. Mixed gland; secretes testosterone & sperms).\n*Sit"}],"Epiploic foramen and lesser sac":[{"src":"Jalal written revision (anatomy)","head":"Q.Epiploic Foramen (Opening to Lesser Sac or Foramen of Winslow)","text":"*Def. & site: Orifice through which lesser sac communicates with greater sac, behind lesser\nomentum free Rt. border, at T12 vertebra level.\n*Boundaries:\n  Ant.  Post.  Sup.  Inf.\n Structures in lesser omentum free\n border; 1-Portal v. (most post.)  -Duodenum 1st inch.\n  I.V.C  Caudate process of liver.\n 2-Hepatic a. (ant. to v. & to Lt. of duct)  -Part of portal v.\n 3-Bile duct (ant. to v. & to Rt. of a.).\n\fModule 206  DIG\n  Peritoneum\n  Peritoneal cavity\n  Epiploic foramen\n\f  Module 206  DIG\n  Stomach\n*Site & shape: In epigastric, umbilical & Lt. hypochondrial regions. Shape modified according to\nbody position & shape. It is horizontal steer horn in supine position & in short stout persons, while\nvertical J-shaped in standing position & in tall slender persons.\n*Ends & curvatures:"},{"src":"Jalal written revision (anatomy)","head":"Q.Lesser Sac (Omental Bursa)","text":"*Def.: Peritoneal cavity part behind stomach & lesser omentum as a result of stomach rotation.\nSeparated from greater sac except at epiploic foramen of Winslow.\n*Extensions: 3; a)Upwards behind liver caudate lobe (sup. recess) b)Downwards between descending\nant. 2 layers & ascending post. 2 layers of greater omentum (inf. recess). c)To Lt. Forming splenic recess\n*Boundaries: 2 walls & 4 borders\n  -Ant. wall;  *Upper border; reflection of peritoneum\n  1-Peritoneum on liver caudate lobe.  from ant. to post. wall of lesser sac.\n  2-Lesser omentum.  *Lower border; greater omentum free lower\n  3- Peritoneumon stomach post.surface.  margin.\n  4-Gastrosplenic ligament.\n  *Lt. border; 1-Meetingof gastrosplenic &\n  5-Greater omentum descending ant. 2 layers.\n  lienorenal ligamentsat spleen hilum\n  2-Greater omentum. Lt. free margin.\n  -Post. wall;\n  1- Stomach bed peritoneal covering.\n  2-Transverse colon & mesocolon.  *Rt. border; 1-Opening into lesser sac\n  3-Greater omentum ascending post. 2 layers.  2-Greater omentum. Rt. free margin."}],"Mesentery of small intestine":[{"src":"Jalal written revision (anatomy)","head":"Q.Small intestine mesentery","text":"*Borders: 2; a)Free border: 6 meters, contains jejunum & ileum coils.\nb)Attached border (root): 6 inches, extending obliquely to Rt. from duodenojejunal\nflexure to ileocaecal junction, crossing: -Duodenum 3rd part\n-Abdominal aorta.  -I.V.C.  -Rt. psoas major  -Rt. genitofemoral n.\n-Rt. gonadal a.  -Rt. ureter.\n*Contents: 1-Small intestine Loops; in free border.\n2-Sup. mesenteric vessels; in root, ileal & jejunal branches in between layers.\n3-Lymph vessels (lacteals); carry chyle.\n4-Mesenteric L.N.'s; 100-150 in 3 rows: a)Small-sized: In free border.\nb)Medium-sized: Between free & attached borders  c)Large-sized: In root.\n5-Sympathetic & parasympathetic plexuses  6-Extraperitoneal tissue & fat.\n  Features of large intestine\n  Taeniae coli  Sacculations (haustrations)  Appendices epiploicae\n3 longitudinal muscle bands, starting at  Colon wall bulges in between  Small peritoneal projections\n  taeniae coli, forming pouches\nappendix base & end in sigmoid colon  filled with fat on large\n  because taeniae coli are shorter\nterminal part. 1 foot shorter than large  than colon. Absent in appendix,\n  intestine surface. Absent in\nintestine. In ascending & descending  rectum & anal canal.  appendix, caecum, rectum &\ncolons, they are ant., posteromed. &  anal canal.\nposterolat., while in transverse colon\nthey are 1 post. & 2 ant. (sup. & inf.)."}],"Caecum and appendix":[{"src":"Jalal written revision (anatomy)","head":"Q.Vermiform appendix","text":"*Site: Lies in Rt. iliac fossa, with base attached to caecum posteromed. aspect, 1 inch below & lat.\nto ileocaecal valve.\n*Surface anatomy: McBurney’s point; appendix base lies at junction of lat. 1/3\nwith med. 2/3 of line from umbilicus to Rt. A.S.I.S.\n*Positions:\n  Preileal or\n  Retrocaecal(75%)  Pelvic (20%)  Subcaecal (3%)  Other rare\n  postileal (2%)\n  -Retrocolic; appendix lies\n  Appendix hangs over  deep to ascending colon.\n  Appendix lies in  Appendix lies below  Appendix in front\n  pelvic brim into  -Subhepatic; appendix lies\n retrocaecal recess.  caecum.  or behind ileum.\n  pelvis.  under liver in congenital\n  short ascending colon.\n*Peritoneal covering: Completely covered by peritoneum & has meso-appendix.\nMesoappendix: Triangular peritoneal fold covering vermiform appendix. It's base is attached to\nsmall intestine mesentery, close to ileocaecal junction. Contains;\na)Vermiform appendix; in Rt. free border.  b)Appendicular vessels; in Lt. free border.\nc) Lymph vessels & L.N.'s.  d)Sympathetic & parasympathetic fibers.\n*Arterial supply: Appendicular a. (end a.) of post. caecal a. or of ileocolic a. It descends behind\nileum terminal part to run in mesoappendix Lt. free border.\n*Venous drainage: Appendicular v. in ileocolic v. (sup. mesenteric v.)."},{"src":"Jalal written revision (anatomy)","head":"Q.Caecum","text":"*Site: In Rt. iliac fossa, above lat. 1/2 of Rt. inguinal ligament.\n*Surface anatomy: Triangular area bounded by; Lat. 1/2 of Rt. inguinal ligament, intertubercular\nplane & Rt. midclavicular line.\n*Caecal orifices: 1)Ileocaecal orifice; into posteromed. aspect of caecum\nupper end, guarded by ileocaecal valve.\n2) Vermiform appendix; into caecum posteromed. aspect, 1 inch below & lat. to ileocaecal valve.\n3) Colic orifice; caecum upper end is continuous with ascending colon.\n*Peritoneal covering: Nearly completely covered by peritoneum (no mesentery).\n*Relations:\n  Anteriorly  Posteriorly\n 1)Ant. abdominal wall.  *3 muscles: -Psoas major -Psoas minor  -Iliacus.\n 2)Greater omentum  *3 nerves: - Genitofemoral -Femoral -Lat. cutaneous n. of thigh.\n  *3 vessels: -External iliac a. -Rt. gonadal a. -Rt. gonadal v.\n 3)Small intestine.\n *Arterial supply: Ant. & post. caecal aa., of ileocolic a. (of sup. mesenteric a.)\n *Venous drainage: Caecal vv. into sup. mesenteric v. (portal circulation).\n\fModule 206  DIG\n  Abdominal organs\n  Small intestine mesentery\n  Large lntestine\n  Parts & features\n  Caecum\n\f  Module 206  DIG"}],"Spleen: surface anatomy, ligaments, relations, blood supply":[{"src":"Jalal written revision (anatomy)","head":"Q.Spleen","text":"*Site: In Lt. hypochondrium, but its post. end extends into epigastric region.\n*Relations : 1-Sup. border: Sharp & marked by one or more notches.\n2-Inf. border: Rounded & smooth.\n3-Med. end: Tapering, directed upwards, backwards & med.\n4-Lat. end: Broad, directed downwards, forwards & lat.\n5-Diaphragmatic surface: Smooth & directed lat. Related to diaphragm which separates it from Lt.\npleura & lung, 9th, 10th & 11th ribs.\n6-Visceral surface: -Directed med. towards abdominal cavity, presents hilum & 4 impressions:\na)Gastric impression: Above hilum, related to stomach fundus.\nb)Renal impression: Below hilum, related to front of Lt. kidney.\nc)Colic impression: Near lat. end, related to Lt. colic flexure.\nd)Pancreatic impression: Just below hilum lat. end, related to pancreas tail.\n-Hilum: Antero-posterior slit between gastric & renal impression. Allows passages of splenic vessels,\nlymphatics & nerves.\n*Surface anatomy: Lies opposite 9th, 10th & 11th ribs. Its med. end lies 1.5 inch from midline post.,\nwhile its lat. end lies in Lt. mid-axillary line.\n*Peritoneal covering: Completely covered by peritoneum. Related to lesser sac splenic recess. Has 3\nperitoneal ligaments;\n  Gastro-splenic ligament  Lieno-renal ligament  Phrenico-colic ligament\n *Extends between upper lip of spleen  *Extends from lower lip of spleen  *Extends between Lt.\n hilum & upper part of stomach greater  hilum to front of Lt. kidney.  colic flexure & diaphragm.\n  curvature.  *Contains: 1-Splenic vessels.\n  *Contains: 1-Short gastric & Lt.  2-Tail of pancreas  *Supports spleen from below.\n  gastroepiploic vessels.  3-Lymphatics &\n  2-Lymphatics & splenic L.N's.  pancreatico-splenic L.N's.\n  3-Extra-peritoneal fat & autonomic  4-Extra-peritoneal fat & autonomic\n  nerve fibers.  fibers.\n  4-Accessory splen"},{"src":"Jalal written revision (anatomy)","head":"Q.Splenic artery","text":"*Origin, course & end: Largest branch of coeliac trunk, wavy course, runs along pancreas upper\nborder, reaching spleen & divides into terminal splenic.\n*Branches:\n  Pancreatic  Short gastric  Left gastro-epiploic  Post. gastric (50%)  Terminal splenic\n  Supplies stomach & greater\n  Segmental end\n  Supply pancreas  Supplies stomach  omentum,  ends  by\n  To fundus.  arteries, enter\n  body & tail.  fundus.  anastomosing  with Rt.\n  spleen hilum.\n  gastro- epiploic a."}],"Portosystemic anastomosis":[{"src":"Jalal written revision (anatomy)","head":"Q.Portosystemic anastomoses","text":"a) Anastomosis at esophagus lower end:\n1-Esophageal tributaries of Lt. gastric vein (portal) 2-Esophageal tributaries of azygos vein (systemic).\nIn portal hypertension, it leads to esophageal varices, which may rupture leading to haematemesis & melena.\nb)Anastomosis at rectum lower end & anal canal upper end:\n1-Superior rectal vein (portal)  2-Middle & inferior rectal veins (systemic).\nIn portal hypertension, it leads to piles & bleeding per rectum.\nc) Anastomosis around umbilicus:\n1-Paraumbilical veins (portal)  2-Sup. & inf. epigastric veins (systemic).\nIn portal hypertension, it leads to Caput Medusae.\nd) Other sites of anastomosis:\n1- Live bare area: Between liver capillaries (portal) & phrenic veins (systemic)\n2-Post. abdominal wall: Between colic veins (portal) & lumbar veins (systemic).\n  Pancreas\n*Type: Mixed gland; -Endocrine; secretes insulin, glucagon & gastrin.\n  -Exocrine; secretes pancreatic juice with digestive enzymes.\n*Q.Site: Retroperitoneal lobulated gland, lies obliquely on post. abdominal wall upper part, in\nepigastrium & left hypochondrium.\n*Q.Parts & relations:\n  Head  Neck  Body  Tail\n Lies within duodenum  Constricted part  Triangular in cross section; 3 borders & 3 surfaces:\n concavity with uncinate  between head &  *Ant. surface: Stomach, separated by lesser sac.\n process projecting to Lt.  body.  *Inf. surface: 1-Duodenojejunal flexure.\n *Ant.: -Head proper:  *Ant.: Pyloro-  2- Jejunum loops.  3-Lt. colic flexure.\n Transverse colon & small  duodenal junction,  *Post. surface: 1-Lt. kidney.  Pancreas Lt.\n intestine loops.  separated by  2-Lt. suprarenal gland 3-Lt. psoas major.  end, running\n -Uncinate process: Sup.  lesser sac.  4-Diaphragm lt. crus 5-Lt. sympathetic chain.  in lienorenal\n mesenteric vessels.  6-Abdominal aorta & sup. mesente"}],"Liver: surface anatomy, bare areas, relations":[{"src":"Jalal written revision (anatomy)","head":"Q.Liver","text":"*Site & lobes: Rt. hypochondrium, epigastrium & may extend in Lt. hypochondrium. Divided into\nlarge Rt. & smaller Lt. lobes by; falciform ligament (on ant. & sup. surfaces), fissure for\nligamentum venosum (on post. surface) & fissure for ligamentum teres (on inf. surface).\n*Surface anatomy: a)Upper border: Line passing through:\n-5th intercostal space in Lt. midclavicular line -Xiphisternal junction.\n-5th rib in Rt. midclavicular line  -7th rib in Rt. midaxillary line.\nb) Rt. border: Line between;\n-7th rib in Rt. midaxillary line  -1cm below costal margin in Rt. midaxillary Iine.\nc) Inf. border: Line passing through;\n-1 cm below costal margin in Rt. midaxillary line\n-9th costal cartilage in Rt. midclavicular line (Murphy's point).\n-Transpyloric plane in midline -Lt. 8th costal cartilage tip.  -5th intercostal space in Lt. midclavicular line.\n*Relations of surfaces:\n  Post. surface  Inf. (visceral) surface\nShows the following from Lt. to Rt.;  Shows the following from Lt. to Rt.;\n1)esophageal groove: On Lt. lobe post. aspect,\n  1)Gastric impression: On Lt. lobe inf.\nrelated to esophagus abdominal part.\n  aspect, related to stomach fundus & body.\n2)Ligamentum venosum fissure: Lodges ligamentum  2)Tuber omentale: Related to lesser\nvenosum (obliterated ductus venosus, connects portal  omentum ant. surface.\nv. Lt. branch to I.V.C. Gives attachment to lesser  3) Ligamentum teres fissure: Obliterated Lt.\nomentum.\n  umbilical v., transmits blood from placenta to\n3)Caudate lobe:  portal v. Lt. branch, passing in falciform\n*On Lt. side: Ligamentum venosum fissure.  ligament free border.\n*On Rt. side: I.V.C groove.  4)Quadrate lobe:\n*Above: Liver sup. surface *Below: Porta hepatis.  *On Lt. side: Ligamentum teres fissure\n-Related to diaphragm, separated from it by lesser sac\n  *On "},{"src":"Jalal written revision (anatomy)","head":"Q.Liver & gall bladder development & anomalies","text":"*Pars Cystica: Forms distal dilated part (gall bladder) & it's proximal stem forms cystic\nduct. Hepatic bud original stem forms common bile duct.\n-Septum transversum will be divided by liver growth into;\n*Caudal region (ventral mesogastrium): Gives rise to lesser omentum, falciform,\ncoronary & triangular ligaments.\n*Central mesenchyme: Gives rise to hematopoietic cells, Kupffer cells & liver CT cells.\n*Cranial region: Gives rise to diaphragm central tendon.\n- Liver size & weight:*By 10th week, liver is about 10% of total body weight\n*At birth, liver from only 5% of total body weight.\n-Congenital anomalies: 1)Biliary atresia; intrahepatic or extrahepatic.\n  2)Accessory hepatic ducts & gall bladder duplication; asymptomatic."}],"Gall bladder":[{"src":"Jalal written revision (anatomy)","head":"Q.Common bile duct                                                    Q.Gall bladder","text":"*Formation: Union of common hepatic duct & cystic duct.  *Site: In gall bladder fossa, on inf. surface of liver Rt. lobe,\n  just to Rt. of quadrate lobe.\n  *Parts;  *Parts & relations:\n  1)Fundus; protrudes below liver inf. border.\n  1)Supraduodenal part: Passes in lesser omentum free  *Ant.: Ant. abdominal wall.  *Post.: Transverse colon.\n  border, in front of portal v. & on hepatic a. Rt. side,\n  separated from IVC behind by epiploic foramen.  2) Body; *Sup.: Liver inf. surface *Inf.: -Transverse colon -\n  End of duodenum 1st part & begin of 2nd part.\n  3) Neck; *Sup.: cystic a. separating it from liver.\n  2)Retroduodenal part: Lies behind 2nd inch of  *Inf.: 2nd inch of duodenum 1st part.\n  duodenum 1st part, with gastroduodenal a. on it's Lt.\n  side & portal vein behind separating them from I.V.C.  *Surface anatomy: Fundus at Rt. 9th costal cartilage tip; where\n  transpyloric plane crosses Rt. costal margin (Murphy's point).\n  *Peritoneal covering: Fundus is completely covered with\n  3)Infraduodenal part: Lies behind pancreas head &  peritoneum, body & neck are only covered on their inf. surface.\n  in front of I.V.C.\n  *Arterial supply: -Cystic a.: Rt. hepatic a. branch, reaches\n  gall bladder by passing in Calot triangle.\n  -Many small aa.: Pass to gall bladder from it's bed in liver.\n  4)Intraduodenal part: Common bile duct unites with  *Venous drainage: Cystic veins; open into hepatic veins or\n  main pancreatic duct, form Vater ampulla which  into portal v. Rt. branch.\n  opens at major duodenal papilla in middle of\n  duodenum 2nd part, surrounded by sphincter of Oddi.  *Lymphatic Drainage: a)Sup. surface lymph vessels;\n  communicate with liver lymph vessels.\n  b)Remaining parts lymph vessels; pass to cystic & hepatic nodes.\n  *N. supply: -Parasympathetic; vagus n"}],"Abdominal aorta and IVC":[{"src":"Jalal written revision (anatomy)","head":"Q.Abdominal aorta","text":"*Beginning: At T12 lower border, as continuation of descending thoracic aorta.\n*End: Passes through aortic opening of diaphragm to end at L4 lower border, by dividing into 2\ncommon iliac aa.\n*Branches: a)Single branches:\n1-Coeliac trunk: Arises opposite T12 lower border or L1 upper border.\n2-Sup. mesenteric a.: Arises opposite L1 lower border\n3-Inf. mesenteric a.: Arises opposite L3\n4-Median sacral a.: Arises from back of aorta lower end at L4.\n  Middle\n  Inf. phrenic  Renal  Gonadal  Lumbar  Common iliac\n  suprarenal\n  Arise at aorta  Arise at L1, supply  Arise at L2 upper  Arise from aorta  4 pairs from aorta  Terminals of aorta.\n  beginning.  suprarenal gland.  border Gives inf.  antero-lat.  back, 5th  Ends at L5 lower\nGives sup. supra-  suprarenal a.  aspect, just below  pair from median  border by dividing\n  renal a.  renal a. (L2).  sacral a.  into external &\n  internal iliac aa.\n\fModule 206  DIG\n  Extrahepatic billary system\n  common bile ducts, Gall bladder\n  Post. abdominal wall vessels\n  Abdominal aorta\n  Beginning, end , relations & single branches\n\f  Module 206  DIG"},{"src":"Jalal written revision (anatomy)","head":"Q.Inferior vena cava (I.V.C)","text":"*Beginning: Union of 2 common iliac veins at L5, behind Rt. common iliac a.\n*End: Pierces diaphragm central tendon opposite T8, 1inch to Rt. of median plane, entering Rt.\natrium lower post. part, opposite Rt. 6th sternocostal junction.\n*Tributaries: 1-2common iliac vv.  2-3rd & 4th lumbar vv. on both sides.\n  3- Rt. gonadal v. (Lt. joins Lt. renal v.)  4-Rt. & Lt. renal vv.\n  5-Rt. suprarenal v. (Lt. joins Lt. renal v.)  6-Rt. & Lt. inf. phrenic vv.\n  7-Rt., Lt. & middle hepatic vv.  8-Vena azygos.\n  Kidney\n*Site: On post. abdominal wall, opposite 12th thoracic & upper 3 lumbar vertebrae. Lt. kidney\nreaches up to 11th rib while Rt. kidney reaches 11th space.\n*Shape & hilum: Bean-shaped;\n-2 ends (upper & lower) -2 surfaces (ant. & post.) -2 borders (Lat. & med.)."}],"Suprarenal glands":[{"src":"Jalal written revision (anatomy)","head":"Q.Suprarenal glands","text":"Right Suprarenal  Left Suprarenal\n  Triangular in shape.  Semilunar in shape.\n  Lies on upper pole of Rt. kidney, not  Lies on upper pole of Lt. kidney,\n  reaching kidney hilum.  reaching kidney hilum.\n  Hilum is directed upwards.  Hilum is directed downwards.\n  Ant.: I.V.C & liver Rt. lobe (bare area).  Ant.: Stomach & lesser sac.\n  Post.: Diaphragm crus  Med.: Coeliac ganglion.\n  Covered by peritoneum inf.  Covered by peritoneum sup.\n  Venous drainage: Rt. suprarenal v. into I.V.C.  Venous drainage: Lt. suprarenal v. into Lt. renal v.\n  Arterial supply: Sup. suprarenal a. (from phrenic a.), middle suprarenal a. (from aorta) &\n  inf. suprarenal a. (from renal a.).\n\fModule 206  DIG\n  Kidney\n  Blood supply\n  Surface anatomy\n  Suprarenal (adrenal) glands\n\f  Module 206  DIG"}],"Psoas major":[{"src":"Jalal written revision (anatomy)","head":"Q.Psoas Major","text":"*Origin: -All lumbar vertebrae transverse processes.\n  -Lumbar bodies & intervertebral discs  -Tendinous arches over lumbar vessels.\n*Insertion: Iliopsoas tendon, into femur lesser trochanter.\n*N. supply: Lumbar plexus (L2, 3, 4).\n*Action: -Main thigh flexor & med rotator -Trunk lat. bending (one psoas).\n  -Trunk forward bending (both psoas)."}],"Quadratus lumborum":[{"src":"Jalal written revision (anatomy)","head":"Q.Quadratus lumborum","text":"*Origin: -Iliolumbar ligament -Med. part of iliac crest.\n*Insertion: -Last rib med. ½ -Upper 4 lumbar transverse processes tips.\n*N. supply: T12 & L1, 2, 3,4.\n*Action: -Fixes last rib, helping diaphragm to contract more effectively\n  -Lat. flexion vertebral column -Acting together, they extend lumbar vertebral column."}],"Stomach: arteries, veins, ligaments, development":[{"src":"Jalal written revision (anatomy)","head":"Q.Stomach development & anomalies","text":"-In 5th week, stomach appears as fusiform dilatation, having;\n  -2 ends: Upper (cardiac) & lower (pyloric) -2 surfaces: Rt. & Lt  -2 borders: Ant. & post.\n  -It is connected to ventral & dorsal body walls by ventral & dorsal mesogastria.\n  -It's shape & position change due to;\n  *Differential growth; post. border grows faster than ant. border:\n  -Post. border becomes greater curvature.  -Ant. border becomes lesser curvature.\n  *90°clockwise rotation around it's longitudinal axis resulting in:\n  -Original Lt. surface becomes ant. surface & original Rt. surface becomes post. surface.\n  -Original Lt. & Rt. vagi become ant. & post. gastric nerves.\n  -Dorsal mesogastrium will be pulled to Lt. forming lesser sac.\n  *Stomach rotates around anterior-post. axis so that; Pyloric end moves to Rt. & upward,\n  while cardiac end moves to Lt. & downward.\n  -Congenital anomalies: Congenital hypertrophic pyloric stenosis; extreme thickening\n  of pyloric sphincter circular smooth muscles, with narrowing of pylorus lumen."}],"Duodenum":[{"src":"Jalal written revision (anatomy)","head":"Q.Duodenum evelopment & anomalies","text":"-1st & 2nd part of duodenum up to common bile duct opening develop from foregut, while\n  2nd part of duodenum below common bile duct opening with 3rd & 4th parts develop from\n  midgut. Duodenal loop appears as forward convex loop with mesentery (mesoduodenum) &\n  hepatic bud is attached to it's ventral wall.\n  -Duodenum position change due to:\n  * Stomach clockwise rotation 90° rotating duodenal loop to Rt.\n  *Differential growth of duodenal walls, so common bile duct will open into duodenum\n  postero-med. wall.\n  -Mesoduodenum absorption: Mesoduodenum Rt. surface fuses with adjacent peritoneum &\n  both layers disappear, so duodenum (except it's 1st inch) become fixed in retroperitoneal\n  position.\n  -During 2nd month, duodenum lumen is obliterated by cells proliferation in it's lumen, then lumen\n  is recanalized shortly after that.\n  -Congenital anomalies: Duodenum stenosis or atresia; due to incomplete recanalization."}],"Midgut development and anomalies":[{"src":"Jalal written revision (anatomy)","head":"Q.Midgut development & anomalies","text":"-Midgut begins caudal to liver bud & extends to junction of Rt. 2/3 & Lt. 1/3 of transverse colon in\nadult. It is connected with post. abdominal wall by mesentery.\n-It elongates forming U-shaped midline loop, with;\n*Apex: Connected with 2ry yolk sac by vitello-intestinal duct.\n*Cephalic limb: Forms duodenum distal part, jejunum & most ileum.\n*Caudal limb: Forms ileum (small), caecum, appendix, ascending colon & transverse colon Rt. 2/3.\n*Axis: Formed by sup. mesenteric a.\n-Physiological umbilical hernia: Intestinal loops leave abdominal cavity & enter umbilical cord\nabout 6th week.\n-Rotation of intestinal loop: In same time in which intestinal loop is elongating, it rotates around axis\nformed by sup. mesenteric a. Herniated intestinal loops begin to return to abdominal cavity during\n10th week of development. Rotation takes place anticlockwise for 270°; 90° during herniation &\n180° during return.\n-Loops return to abdominal cavity in special order; jejunum proximal part is 1st part to enter abdomen\nand lies on Lt. side, next returning loops gradually lie more to Rt. side. Caecal swelling, is the last to\nreturn lying below liver Rt. lobe, then it descends into Rt. iliac fossa, forming Rt. colic flexure &\nascending colon. Reduction is due to; regression of mesonephric kidney, reduced liver growth &\nabdominal cavity expansion.\n-When ascending & descending colon obtain their definitive positions, their mesenteries press against\npost. abdominal wall peritoneum, so they become retroperitoneal.\n\fModule 206  DIG\n  Derivatives of forgut caudal part\n  c)Liver &gall bladder development\n  Midgut development\n\f  Module 206  DIG"}],"Small intestine (Paneth, M cells, villi)":[{"src":"Histology revision (Yousof Yasser)","head":"‫‪HISTOLOGY 206 REVISION‬‬","text":"‫اللهم علمنا ما ينفعنا وانفعنا بما علمتنا وزدنا بك عل ًما‬\n  ‫قد يحتوي هذا الملف على أخطاء‬\n  ‫‪Yousof Yasser‬‬\n\f  Microvilli\nStructure  Cell  Description\nTongue (Taste  Dark Supporting Cells  Feature long microvilli projecting into the taste\nBuds)  pore.\nTongue (Taste  Light Gustatory (Taste)  Feature microvilli at their apical surface.\nBuds)  Cells\nStomach  Oxyntic (Parietal) Cells  Intracellular canaliculi are lined with numerous\n(Fundus)  microvilli.\nStomach  Enteroendocrine Cells  Feature few apical microvilli to monitor gastric\n(Fundus)  contents.\nSmall Intestine  Enterocytes  Apical microvilli form a brush border that\n  (Absorptive)  increases surface area 20x.\nSmall Intestine  Enteroendocrine Cells  Feature few apical microvilli to monitor intestinal\n  contents.\nLiver  Hepatocytes  Long microvilli face blood sinusoids; short\n  microvilli face bile canaliculi.\nGall Bladder  Lining Epithelium  Simple columnar cells possess apical microvilli.\nPCT  Lining Epithelium  Many long microvilli form a prominent apical\n  brush border.\nDCT  Lining Epithelium  few and short microvilli compared to the PCT.\nCollecting  Principal Cells  scanty, short microvilli and a single cilium.\nDuct\nCollecting  Intercalated Cells  many apical microvilli.\nDuct\n\f  Basal striations\nOrgan/Structure  Specific Location  Description in Text"},{"src":"Zahra histology notes","head":"Cell                    ❶ Absorptive Columnar cells                ❷ Undifferentiated Stem or","text":"Type  (Enterocytes)  (Regenerative) cells\nSite y Villi & crypts  y Found mainly at base of crypt\n  y Tall columnar cells with basal oval nuclei.  y Low columnar cells.\nL.M.\n  y Apical brush border.  y Basal oval nuclei & prominent nucleoli.\n  y Apical microvilli.  y Few Organelles.\n  y Covered by cell coat rich in enzymes that y Polyribosomes.\n  help terminal digestion (disaccharidases &\n  dipeptidases)\n  y Lateral borders show junctional complexes\n  acting as a barrier between intestinal\n  lumen & intercellular compartment.\n  y Supranuclear Golgi, rER, mitochondria &\n  few free ribosomes.\n  y sER for absorption of lipids.\nE.M.\n  y Terminal digestion of CHO & proteins.  y Renewal of other cell types as they\nFunction\n  y Absorption  proliferate, differentiate & migrate to\n  villi & crypts.\n  20  HISTOLOGY - Dr AHMED ZAHRA\n  Chapter\n  GIT\nCell Type  ❸ Goblet Cells  ❹ Paneth Cells\n  y Villi & crypts  y At the base of small intestinal crypts only.\n  Site  y Their number increases from\n  duodenum to ilium.\n  • Shape: Expanded flask shaped. • Shape: Pyramidal cells\n  • Nucleus:Narrow cylindrical  • Nucleus: basal rounded."},{"src":"Zahra histology notes","head":"the exterior by the anal canal.","text":"Cecum and Colon\n They are similar in structure.\n They form most of the length of the large intestine.\n24  HISTOLOGY - Dr AHMED ZAHRA\n  Chapter\n  GIT\n Histological differences between small & large intestines\n  Small Intestine  Large Intestine\n  y Villi & crypts  y Crypts only (wide & deep).\n Mucosa:\n  y No villi.\n  a)  y Columnar absorptive, goblet,  y The crypts: are lined by absorptive\nEpithelium  stem, Paneth, APUD.  columnar cells, numerous goblet\n  cells (increase in number from the\n  cecum to the sigmoid colon), few\n  (APUD) cells and some basal stem\n  cells.\n  y There are no Paneth cells.\n  b)  y Contains lymphocytes, plasma  y C.T. layer rich in lymphoid cells\n Lamina  cells & leukocytes between the  and nodules (follicles).\n propria  crypts.\n  c)\nMuscularis  y Formed of inner circular and outer longitudinal smooth muscle layers\nmucosae\n  y Contains Brunner's glands in  y C.T. layer containing blood\n  duodenum & Peyer's patches in  vessels, nerves and lymphatics.\nSubmucosa\n  ileum.  y It contains neither glands nor\n  lymph follicles."}],"Salivary glands and serous/mucous acini":[{"src":"Zahra histology notes","head":"GIT","text":"❷ The Submandibular Gland\n  A pair of glands situated at the inner side of the mandible.\nStructure:\n 1. The stroma:\n   Thick capsule.\n   Thin C.T. trabeculae, dividing the gland into lobes & lobules.\n 2. The Parenchyma:\n   The submandibular gland is a seromucous gland.\n   In humans, 90% of its acini are serous and remaining 10% are mucous &\n  mucoserous acini.\n   In dog, mucous acini predominate.\n   Structure of acini: Refer to the types of secretory acini in exocrine glands.\n   The duct system\n  9 the same as that of all major salivary glands.\n  9 The main duct (Wharton's duct) opens on the floor of the mouth cavity.\n  HISTOLOGY - Dr AHMED ZAHRA  33\n  Chapter\n  GIT\n  ❸ The Sublingual Gland\n  A pair of glands present under oral mucosa of the floor of mouth.\nStructure:\n 1. The stroma:\n   Thin C.T. capsule\n   Thick C.T. septa dividing gland into lobes & lobules.\n 2. The parenchyma:\n   Sublingual gland is a mucoserous gland\n   Mucous acini predominate with mixed mucoserous acini, but no pure serous acini.\n   Structure of acini: refer to the types of secretory acini.\n   The main duct often joins the submandibular main duct or may open"},{"src":"Zahra histology notes","head":" Hemorrhoids (piles): A condition occurs when the venous plexuses; internal","text":"(above pectinate line) or external (at the anus) become, distended and protrude\n  from anus → bleeding during bowel movements.\n  Anal Fissure:\n  • a crack in anus and anal canal, caused by trauma.\n  • The symptom of anal fissure is pain during and following bowel movements\n  ❷ THE DIGESTIVE GLANDS\nThey include the salivary glands, the pancreas and the liver.\n  A. Salivary Glands\n They are exocrine glands that produce saliva.\n Saliva contains bactericidal protective substances such as immunoglobulin A\n  (IgA), lysozyme and bacteriostatic substances [lactoferrin].\n Types of Salivary Glands\n  A. Major Salivary Glands:\n  9 They are compound tubulo-alveolar exocrine glands.\n  9 They secrete 90% of the total volume of the saliva in humans.\n  9 Major salivary glands are paired & include:\n  1. The parotid glands: in front of both ears.\n  2. The submandibular glands: lie at the inner side of the mandible.\n  3. The sublingual glands: lie at the floor of the oral cavity.\n  B. Minor Salivary Glands:\n  9 Branched tubulo-alveolar glands.\n  9 They secrete 10% of the total volume of saliva.\n  9 Scattered in lamina propria of oral mucosa of:\n  1. lips [labial],\n  2. cheeks [buccal],\n  3. palate [palatine], tonsils,\n  4. tongue [lingual and von Ebner's glands].\n  HISTOLOGY - Dr AHMED ZAHRA  29\n  Chapter"},{"src":"Zahra histology notes","head":"❶ THE PAROTID GLAND (the largest major salivary gland)","text":"Structure:\n I. STROMA: formed of:\n  A) Capsule: Thick, formed of C.T. cells & fibers, rich in fat cells.\n  B) Trabeculae: [septa]\n  • Thick, formed of C.T. cells & fibers,\n  • Descend from the capsule to divide the gland into lobes and lobules.\n  • Carry blood vessels, nerves and ducts.\n  C) Reticular Stroma:\n  • Present in the background, formed of reticular cells & fibers.\n  • Can be demonstrated with silver (Ag).\n  N.B. An IgA- rich secretory complex is formed of\n  • IgA: [produced by plasma cells in the CT stroma]\n  • a secretory component [formed by the serous cells, intercalated ducts &\n  striated ducts].\n  • This complex is resistant to digestion, and defend against pathogens in the\n  oral cavity.\n  HISTOLOGY - Dr AHMED ZAHRA  31\n Chapter\n  GIT\n II. PARENCHYMA: It consists of:\n  A. The Serous Acini:\n  • Structure:\n  9 They have small diameter and narrow lumen.\n  9 Lined with serous cells:\n  ; By LM: Structure of acini: refer to the types of secretory acini in exocrine glands.\n  ; By EM:\n  - Adjacent interdigitating cell membranes are joined together by\n  junctional complexes.\n  - Rich in r-ER, well developed Golgi apparatus, numerous mitochondria"}],"Hepatocytes, liver lobule and acinus":[{"src":"Zahra histology notes","head":"❸ Liver Acinus","text":" Definition:\n  - A diamond shaped mass of liver cells including parts from two adjacent classic\n  hepatic lobules.\n  - It has a central vascular core (formed of a branch of hepatic artery and a portal\n  vein that arise from the vessels in portal area).\n It is divided into three zones:\n  1. Zone I:\n  9 lies close to vascular core.\n  9 It is rich in blood supply\n  9 First to receive glucose &\n  oxygen.\n  2. Zone II:\n  9 Intermediate zone,\n  9 Less blood supply than\n  zone I.\n  3. Zone III:\n  9 at the periphery of the liver acinus.\n  9 It is far from the vascular core and the nearest to central veins.\n  9 It is the poorest in blood supply\n  9 Least amount of glucose & oxygen.\n The zone arrangement of the liver acinus explains the variation in liver cell damage\n  in response to hypoxia and toxins.\n Cells of zone III (the poorest in blood supply) are more liable to damage by\n  hypoxia,\n Cells of zone I (the nearest to the vascular core) are more affected by toxins due to\n  their rich blood supply.\n  HISTOLOGY - Dr AHMED ZAHRA  43\n  Chapter\n  GIT"},{"src":"Zahra histology notes","head":"HISTOLOGY - Dr AHMED ZAHRA     39","text":"Chapter\n  GIT\n  ❶ Classic Hepatic Lobule\n Hexagonal mass of liver tissue draining their venous blood into a central vein.\n Demarcated by thin C.T. septa.\n Liver cells (hepatocytes) are:\n  • arranged in cords or plates radiating from the central vein\n  • Separated with blood sinusoids.\n  • Each cord is formed of 2 rows enclosing a bile canaliculus.\n  • Blood sinusoids are separated from hepatocytes by space of Disse.\nLiver Cells (Hepatocytes):\n  LM:\n  1. Shape: Large polygonal cell\n  2. Nuclei: Central, rounded & pale (some cells are\n  binucleated) with prominent nucleoli.\n  3. Cytoplasm:\n  9 Acidophilic (due to mitochondria & sER),\n  9 vacuolated (due to dissolved glycogen).\n  9 Glycogen can be stained with PAS or Best's Carmine.\n  EM:\n  1. The cell membrane:\n  a) Facing the blood sinusoids: shows long microvilli (help the transfer of\n  materials between hepatocytes & blood).\n  b) Facing the bile canaliculus: shows short microvilli\n  c) Facing neighboring hepatocytes: shows junctional complexes.\n40  HISTOLOGY - Dr AHMED ZAHRA\n  Chapter\n  GIT\n  2. Cytoplasm: Hepatocytes are very active cells, so they are very rich in:"},{"src":"Histology revision (Yousof Yasser)","head":"gelatin carmine Injection     Blood Sinusoids To demonstrate the radially arranged","text":"blood spaces in the liver.\n  Junctional Complex\n  Interdigitating cells\n  Cell  comment\n  2 acini (Pancreas and Parotid)\n  2 cyte (Hepatocyte and Enterocyte)  No I.D were mentioned in hepatocyte\n  Umbrella (Dome) cell  No I.D were mentioned\n  2 Tubules (PCT and DCT)  No J.C were mentioned\n  Some substances\n  Name  Cell\n  Iga  Saliva -> By plasma CT cell\n  Lysozyme  Paneth and saliva\n  Pepsinogen – rennin – lipase  Peptic chief cell\n  Renin  JG cell\n  Medullipin l  Interstitial cell in kidney\n  (activated in liver)\n\fDiameter comparison\n  Description  structure\nNarrow lumen + small diameter  Serous acini\nNarrow lumen + Large diameter  PCT\nNarrow lumen + Stellate shape  Appendix\n  Glands\n  Structure  Type\n  Fundic glands  Simple branched tubular\n  Pyloric glands  Coiled branched tubular\n  Leiberkuhein crypts  Simple tubular\nMajor salivary gland + Pancreas  Compound tubulo-alveolar\n  Minor salivary glands  Simple branched tubule-alveolar\n  Liver + Kidney  Compound tubular"}],"Urinary passages (ureter, bladder)":[{"src":"Zahra histology notes","head":" Wall of ureter is formed of:","text":"❶ Mucosa:\n  - Shows prominent longitudinal folds giving lumen a stellate appearance.\n  - Formed of uroepithelium (transitional epithelium) & underlying C.T. corium.\n  - Uroepithelium is formed of 3 layers:\n  i. Basal layer: One layer of small cells resting on thin basement membrane.\n  ii. Intermediate layer: One to several layers of polyhedral cells.\n  iii. Superficial layer: Large dome shaped cells (called \"umbrella cells\"), may be\n  binucleated.\n  ❷ Musculosa:\n  - Upper 2/3 of ureter has an inner longitudinal and an outer circular layer of\n  smooth muscles.\n  - Lower 1/3 has an additional outer longitudinal layer (inner longitudinal -\n  middle circular - outer longitudinal).\n  - Contraction of muscle produces peristaltic waves which propel urine towards\n  urinary bladder.\n  ❸ Adventitia: Fibroelastic C.T. has blood vessels, nerves and lymphatics.\n  HISTOLOGY - Dr AHMED ZAHRA  61\n Chapter\n  URINARY SYSTEM\n  Urinary bladder\n ❶ Mucosa:\n  a. Uroepithelium: formed of uroepithelium in which umbrella cells are well\n  developed and have extensive intercellular junctional complex forming a\n  unique apical membrane as contact with urine is greatest.\n   In empty bladder: epithelium is 5-6 layers thick. Superficial cells \"umbrella\n  cells\" are dome shaped.\n   In full bladder: epithelium is\n  3-4 layers thick. Superficial cells\n  become squamous."},{"src":"Histology revision (Yousof Yasser)","head":"Place                                         shape","text":"Esophagus  Longitudinal\n  Stomach  Longitudinal Branched (rugae)\n  Small intestine  Circular (Plica cicrularis)\n  Has submucosal core\n  Anal canal  Long (columns of morgagni)\n  Gall bladder  Highly folded\n  Ureter  Long with stellate shape lumen\n  Lymphoid follicles\n  Place  shape\n  Ilium  Payer’s patches\n  Colon  Mucosa lamina propria\n  Nasopharynx  Pharyngeal tonsil\n  Oropharynx  Palatine tonsil\n  Appendix  Mucosa + Submucosa (around lumen)\n\f  Adventitia\n  Place  shape\n  Esophagus  Above diaphragm\n Gall bladder  Only at hepatic surface\nUrinary bladder  All except upper surface\n  Ureter  All\n  Rectum  All"},{"src":"Zahra histology notes","head":"pyramids.","text":" Interlobar arteries ascends at columns of\n  Bertin and at corticomedullary junction give\n  off arcuate arteries which arch around the\n  base of the pyramids.\n Arcuate arteries: give rise to small\n  interlobular arteries that enter the cortical\n  tissue in-between its lobules.\n Interlobular arteries: situated between\n  medullary rays & give off afferent arterioles.\n Afferent glomerular arterioles: form the\n  glomerulus and leave it as efferent arteriole.\n Efferent arterioles:\n  - Leave the glomerulus and break up into peritubular capillaries → supplying\n  convoluted tubules.\n  - Endothelial cells lining peritubular capillaries secrete erythropoietin that\n  stimulates erythropoiesis in bone marrow.\n  - Efferent arterioles of JG nephrons give rise to straight arteriolae reactae that\n  extend into medullary pyramids to supply it and collecting tubule.\n Vasa Recta: is formed by arteriola recta and venula recta at the corticomedullary\n junction. It supplies the juxtamedullary nephron. These vessels drain into interlobular\n veins or arcuate veins, then into interlobar veins which form renal vein at the hilum.\n60  HISTOLOGY - Dr AHMED ZAHRA\n  Chapter\n  URINARY SYSTEM\n  URINARY PASSAGES\n They include calyces, renal pelvis, ureters, urinary bladder and urethra.\n Each has a mucosa consisting of uroepithelium (transitional epithelium) and lamina\n  propria of fibroelastic C.T., musculosa and adventitia.\n  URETERS"}],"Tongue and lingual papillae":[{"src":"Zahra histology notes","head":"sulcus terminals, with the apex of the V pointing posteriorly.","text":" Tongue consists of dorsal & ventral surfaces, is covered by oral mucosa.\n  Dorsal surface  Ventral surface\n  Surface  Rough  Smooth\n  • Partially keratinized stratified  • Non-keratinized stratified\n Covering  squamous epithelium  squamous epithelium\nEpithelium • firmly adherent to underlying  • loosely adherent to underlying\n  lamina propria.  lamina propria.\n  Papillae  • Anterior two-thirds shows papillae. • No tongue papillae\n  Special  • Lingual glands are embedded\n  • Posterior one-third shows lingual\n  in ventral portion of the\n  Features  tonsils.\n  muscles of tongue.\n 4  HISTOLOGY - Dr AHMED ZAHRA\n  Chapter\n  GIT\n  The Tongue (Lingual) Papillae\n Definition: elevations of the oral epithelium and lamina propria on the anterior 2/3\n  of dorsum of tongue.\n There are four types of tongue papillae:\n  1-Filiform papillae  2-Fungiform papillae\n  Shape  • Elongated conical shape and  • Resemble mushrooms (narrow\n  slightly curved.  stalk and a smooth-surfaced,\n  dilated upper part).\n  Covering • Keratinized stratified squamous  • thin non-keratinized stratified\n  epithelium  squamous\n  Number  • Most numerous  • Fewer\n  Site  • Anterior 2/3 of dorsum of the  • Irregularly scattered among the\n  tongue.  filiform papillae."},{"src":"Zahra histology notes","head":"squamous overlying a looser type of dense irregular collagenous C.T.","text":" The oral region includes:\n  ❶ The Lips (4)\n Made up of the orbicularis oris skeletal muscle (circumorally) intermixed with dense\n  fibroelastic CT.\n The outer surface: covered by hairy skin with hair follicles, sebaceous & sweat glands.\n The inner surface:\n  • lined by the oral mucosa.\n  • The underlying C.T. contains labial glands, which are predominantly mucous acini.\n The red line (vermilion):\n  • Is a transition zone between the epidermis of skin and oral mucosa.\n  • It is a hairless very thin skin with deep papillae.\n  • Its epidermis is transparent, so it shows the red color of the blood capillaries\n  present in dermis.\n  • It is highly sensitive due to its rich sensory innervation.\n   N.B.: lip margin is devoid of sweat and sebaceous glands; it requires\n  continuous moistening by saliva to prevent cracking.\n  HISTOLOGY - Dr AHMED ZAHRA  3\n  Chapter\n  GIT\n  ❷ The Tongue\n Highly mobile muscular organ.\n It consists of:\n  • striated muscles arranged (in 3 directions) vertically, horizontally and\n  longitudinally at right angles.\n  • This arrangement ensures a higher degree of mobility for mastication, phonation\n  and swallowing.\n The anterior 2/3 and the posterior 1/3 of the tongue are of different embryological\n  origins\n The boundary between the two is marked by a shallow V-shaped groove, the"}],"Oesophagus, tongue, colon, appendix":[{"src":"Zahra histology notes","head":"Taste bud    • No taste buds                      • Contains scattered taste buds","text":"Special  • Whitish in color  • Red appearance due to the\n  features  richly vascularized C.T.\n  3-Circumvallate papillae  4-Foliate papillae\n  Shape  • Each papilla resides in mucosa  • Two or more parallel ridges and\n  and is surrounded by a circular  furrows on the dorso-lateral\n  furrow (valley).  surface of the tongue.\n  Covering • non-keratinized stratified  • non-keratinized stratified\n  epithelium  squamous epith  squamous epith.\n  • Largest and least common type  • Poorly developed in humans,\n  Number  (6-14).  well developed in rabbit &\n  monkey.\n  • V-shaped region in front of  • Dorso-lateral surface of the\n  Site\n  sulcus terminalis.  tongue.\n  Taste bud  • Present at sides of papillae  • Many taste buds.\n  • Numerous serous (von Ebner's)  • Serous glands in the lamina\n  glands drain their secretion  propria discharge their\n  into the deep groove (valley)  secretion via ducts opening\n  Special\n  that encircles periphery of each  into the base of the furrows.\n  features\n  papilla, providing a continuous\n  flow of fluid over great number\n  of taste buds.\n  HISTOLOGY - Dr AHMED ZAHRA  5\n  Chapter\n  GIT\n6  HISTOLOGY - Dr AHMED ZAHRA\n  Chapter"},{"src":"Zahra histology notes","head":"GIT","text":"Taste Buds:\n They are onion-shaped pale neuroepithelial structures.\n They have wide bases and narrow apices.\n They are present on the tongue papillae (circumvallate, fungiform & foliate\n  papillae), soft palate, epiglottis and pharynx.\n The narrow apices of the taste buds have a small opening in the superficial layer\n  called taste pore.\n Each taste bud is formed of the following cells:\n  1. Dark supporting or sustentacular cells:\n  • At the periphery of the taste buds.\n  • Slender dark cells with central nuclei\n  • They have long microvilli projecting into the taste pore.\n  • Secrete a glycoprotein material that surrounds the microvilli in the taste pore.\n  2. Light gustatory or neuroepithelial taste cells:\n  • centrally located cells\n  • Light elongated with central nuclei\n  • Microvilli at their apical surface.\n  • Contain dense-core vesicles in cytoplasm that contain neurotransmitter\n  • The base of taste cells is surrounded by non-myelinated nerve fibers that carry\n  the impulses of taste sensation.\n  3. Basal cells:\n  • Present at the base of the taste bud.\n  • They act as stem cells for renewal of taste cells and supporting cells.\n  HISTOLOGY - Dr AHMED ZAHRA  7\n  Chapter\n  GIT\n  ❸ The Palate\n The palate is divided into:\n  • an anterior hard palate (possessing a bone in its core)"},{"src":"Zahra histology notes","head":"• Lamina propria of posterior aspect of nasopharynx houses pharyngeal tonsil.","text":"HISTOLOGY - Dr AHMED ZAHRA  9\n  Chapter\n  GIT\n  B. Gastrointestinal Tract (GIT) (Alimentary Tract)\n  Composed of esophagus, stomach, small intestine, large intestine and anal canal.\n  ❶ Esophagus\n straight, long, muscular tube that transports the ingested food from the pharynx\n  to the stomach.\n It lies in the thorax, but it pierces the diaphragm to join the stomach.\n It consists of 4 layers, from inside outwards:\n  A. MUCOSA: it shows longitudinal folds and consists of:\n  ❶ Epithelium:\n  - Non-keratinized stratified squamous.\n  - It protects against injury, which may occur due to passage of rough food.\n  ❷ Lamina propria: loose C.T. which contains:\n  - blood vessels and lymphatics that supply the mucosa\n  - mucous glands (cardiac glands), in the upper & lower ends of the\n  esophagus.\n  ❸ Muscularis mucosa:\n  - inner circular (I.C.) & outer longitudinal (O.L.) thin layers of smooth muscle.\n  - Its contraction causes localized folding and movements of the mucosa\n  which is independent of the peristaltic movement.\n  B. SUBMUCOSA:\n  - Consists of loose C.T., blood vessels, lymphatics & Meissner's plexus of\n  nerve fibers which supplies the muscularis mucosa.\n  - It contains mucous glands (esophageal glands);\n  9 their ducts pierce the mucosa to open into lumen.\n  9 Their secretion protects esophageal mucosa.\n  C. MUSCULOSA (Muscularis Externa):"}],"Gastric glands (peptic, parietal, fundic cells)":[{"src":"Zahra histology notes","head":"Corium            • Contains fundic glands          • Contains pyloric glands","text":"• Very crowded  • Less crowded,\n  • long occupy most of thickness  • short, occupy 1/2 the thickness of\n  of mucosa.  mucosa.\n  • Simple branched tubular glands. • Coiled branched tubular glands.\n  • Perpendicular to the surface.  • Not perpendicular to surface.\n  • Minimal C.T. between glands  • Abundant C.T. between glands.\n  • Gastric pits are narrow  • Gastric pits wide & deep.\n  • (1/4- 1/3 of gland length).  • (Pit: gland ratio = 1:1)\n  • Lined by surface columnar,  • Lined by surface columnar,\n  mucous, peptic, oxyntic, APUD  mucous, few APUD & occasional\n  & stem cells.  oxyntic cells.\n  Glands\nSubmucosa • Wider  • Narrower\n  • Thinner  • Thicker\n  • Three layers: inner oblique,  • Two layers: inner thick circular\nMusculosa\n  middle circular and outer  (pyloric sphincter) and outer\n  longitudinal.  longitudinal.\n16  HISTOLOGY - Dr AHMED ZAHRA\n  Chapter\n  GIT\nChanges at the Gastro-Esophageal Junction\n1. The epithelium of the esophagus (stratified squamous epithelium), changes to\n  simple columnar mucous secreting epithelium in the gastric part.\n2. The corium of the stomach is wider and contains gastric glands.\n3. The muscularis mucosae passes unchanged.\n4. The submucosa of the esophagus contains mucous glands then it disappears in\n  the submucosa of stomach.\n5. The musculosa is thicker in the stomach, being formed of three layers: inner"},{"src":"Zahra histology notes","head":"glucose.","text":"surface epithelium.\n  9 D- Cells produce Somatostatin, (antigrowth\n  • Downwards deep to the\n  hormone).\n  gland to replace oxyntic,\n  9 E- Cells produce endorphin (pain killer).\n  peptic & APUD cells.\n  9 EC- Cells produce serotonin (Vasoconstrictor).\n  ❷ SUBMUCOSA: loose C.T. containing large blood vessels, lymphatics and\n  Meissner's parasympathetic nerve plexus supplying muscularis mucosa.\n  ❸ MUSCULOSA:\n  - Smooth muscle fibers, arranged into 3 layers: Inner oblique. - Middle circular. -\n  Outer longitudinal.\n  - Auerbach's nerve plexus is found between the circular & longitudinal layers\n  controlling movements of the musculosa.\n  ❹ SEROSA: loose C.T. covered by mesothelium = simple squamous epithelium.\n  HISTOLOGY - Dr AHMED ZAHRA  15\n  Chapter\n  GIT\n Peptic ulcer: Erosion of the mucous membranes of the stomach or duodenum\n  produced by the action of HCI.\n Pernicious anemia: Lack of intrinsic factor is the most common cause of vitamin\n  B12 deficiency leading to subsequent anemia. It is caused by:\n  ❶ Loss of gastric epithelium in gastrectomy and in healed peptic ulcer.\n  ❷ Some long-term use of drugs used in the treatment of peptic ulcer\n  Pylorus of Stomach\nHistological differences between fundus and pylorus of the stomach\n  Fundus  Pylorus\n Mucosa  • Highly folded, darkly stained  • Less folded, lightly stained"},{"src":"Zahra histology notes","head":"a. Epithelium: simple columnar epithelium.","text":"b. Lamina propria (C.T. corium): loose C.T. containing fundic glands.\n  c. Muscularis mucosae: thin layer of smooth muscle fibers, arranged as inner\n  circular (IC) and outer longitudinal (OL) layers.\n Fundic Glands: They are characterized by:\n  • Simple branched tubular glands\n  • They occupy most of the mucosal thickness.\n  • Very crowded, with little amount of C.T. in-between.\n  • Arranged perpendicular to the surface.\n  • Open into the lumen by short, narrow (gastric pits), in which 2 or 3 glands open.\n  • Pit/secretory portion = 1:3.\n  • Each fundic gland is formed of: isthmus, neck & body.\n12  HISTOLOGY - Dr AHMED ZAHRA\n  Chapter\n  GIT\n  The following are cells lining the fundic mucosa\nType  ❶ Surface mucus cells  ❷ Mucus neck cells\nSite • Gastric pits & isthmus  • Neck of glands\n  • Shape: Tall columnar cells  • Shape: Low columnar cells\n  • Nucleus: basal oval nuclei.  • Nucleus: basal flat nuclei.\n  • Cytoplasm:  • Cytoplasm: Vacuolated\n  9 Apical vacuolated cytoplasm  9 Mucin secretory granules\n  9 Mucin secretory granules stain  9 stain red with PAS reaction.\n  9 red with PAS reaction\nL.M.\n  • Prominent supranuclear Golgi complex. • Prominent supranuclear Golgi complex.\n  • rER, mitochondria.  • Basal rER, mitochondria.\n  • Apical secretory granules.  • Secretory granules\nE.M\n  • Secretion of mucus which forms a  • Secretion of mucus to protect the"}],"Pyloro-duodenal and other junctions":[{"src":"Zahra histology notes","head":"Changes at the Recto- anal Junction","text":"❶ Anal mucosa consists of lining epithelium, lamina propria & muscularis mucosa\n  1. Epithelium is divided into 3 zones:\n  • The upper third (Rectal zone) is lined by simple columnar epithelium.\n  • The middle third (transitional zone)\n  9 at the level of anal columns\n  9 a transition zone lined by stratified columnar epithelium.\n  • The lower third (Squamous zone)\n  9 at the level of pectinate line\n  9 Lined with non- keratinized stratified squamous epithelium.\n  2. Lamina propria:\n  • Composed of fibroelastic C.T.\n  • At the orifice, it contains sebaceous & circumanal apocrine glands & large veins.\n  3. Muscularis mucosa: Composed of an inner circular layer and an outer\n  longitudinal layer of smooth muscle that is broken at the level of pectinate line &\n  then disappears.\n❷ Anal submucosa:\n  • Composed of fibroelastic connective tissue.\n  • It houses two venous plexuses, internal hemorrhoidal plexus, situated above\n  pectinate line, and external hemorrhoidal plexus, located at the anus.\n❸ Anal musculosa:\n  • consists of inner circular & outer longitudinal smooth muscles.\n  • The inner circular layer becomes thickened to form internal anal sphincter.\n  • The outer longitudinal layer of the muscularis externa forms a uniform sheet\n  that passes between the internal & external sphincters.\n❹ The external anal sphincter is formed by striated muscle of the pelvic floor. The\n  external sphincter is under voluntary control.\n28  HISTOLOGY - Dr AHMED ZAHRA\n  Chapter\n  GIT"}],"Pancreas (exocrine/endocrine)":[{"src":"Zahra histology notes","head":"N.B. Pancreatic exocrine secretion is controlled by hormones secreted","text":"by entero- endocrine cells of GIT.\n36  HISTOLOGY - Dr AHMED ZAHRA\n  Chapter\n  GIT\n  b) Duct system:\n  9 Intercalated ducts:\n  • The first duct which arise from the acini,\n  • Lined with simple squamous epithelium.\n  • They telescope into the acinar lumen, so in transverse section they\n  appear lining the lumen (the centroacinose cells).\n  9 Few intra-lobar ducts.\n  9 Inter-lobar ducts: lined with simple cubical epithelium.\n  9 Inter-lobar ducts: lined with simple columnar epithelium.\n  9 Main pancreatic duct: lined with columnar cells, goblet cells & few\n  entero-endocrine cells.\n2. Endocrine Portion (Islets of Langerhans):\n   They are pale areas scattered between the darkly stained pancreatic acini.\n   Their number is about one million, mainly at the tail region.\n   They are not capsulated but supported with reticular stroma.\n   They are formed of cell masses & cords of cells, separated with fenestrated\n  blood capillaries.\n   Cells of Islets of Langerhans:\n  1. Beta cells (B) or (β)  2. Alpha cells (A) or (α)\n  Percentage y - More numerous (70%)  y - less numerous (15%).\n  Size  y - Smaller cells.  y - Larger cells.\n  Site  y - Centrally located  y - peripherally located\n  secretory  y - Alcohol soluble, but  y - water soluble, but\n  granules  water resistant  alcohol Resistant.\n  EM  y rER, well developed GA, electron-dense granules."},{"src":"Zahra histology notes","head":"y Secrete insulin which       y Secrete glucagon","text":"Function:  lowers blood glucose  hormone which increases\n  and helps glycogenesis.  blood sugar.\n  3. Delta cells (D):\n  9 They represent 10% of islet cells.\n  9 Located at the periphery of the islets.\n  9 They contain membrane bound granules which stain only with silver.\n  9 Functions: Secrete somatostatin [anti-growth hormone] which controls\n  the release of insulin & glucagon.\n  4. C-cells:\n  9 Very few (3%), contain few organelles & few secretory granules.\n  9 Function: unknown yet but may represent immature or degranulated cells.\n  HISTOLOGY - Dr AHMED ZAHRA  37\n  Chapter\n  GIT\n  5. PP (F):\n  9 Very few.\n  9 They contain few organelles & few granules.\n  9 Function: Secrete a pancreatic polypeptide to reduce pancreatic secretion.\n  6. G-cells: secrete gastrin.\n  7. Ganglion cells: nerve cells, for autonomic nervous control of the islets.\n  Differences between parotid gland and pancreas:\n  Parotid gland  Pancreas\n  Number & Type  y Two exocrine glands  y Single mixed gland\n  Capsule  y Thick, rich in fat cells.  y Thin, delicate\n  y Covered with skin  y No fat. Covered with peritoneum\n  Trabeculae  y Thick, clear lobulation  y Thin, delicate\n  y non- clear lobulation\n  y Basal basophilia and apical  y More prominent basal\n  Cytoplasm"},{"src":"Zahra histology notes","head":"acidophilic                     basophilia & apical acidophilia","text":"Acini:\n  Myoepithelium y Present  y Absent\n  Centroacinose y Absent  y Present\n  cells\nIntercalated Striated y Not telescoped  y Telescoped\n duct (intralobular) y Many  y Few\n  Interlobar ducts  y Lined by pseudostratified  y Lined by simple columnar cells\n  columnar cells\n  Main duct  y Lined by stratified columnar y Lined by columnar cells,\n  then stratified squamous  goblet cells & few entero-\n  epithelium  endocrine cells\nIslets of Langerhans y Absent  y Present\n  Type I Diabetes mellitus [juvenile-dependent]\n  • Occurs before the age of 20 [juvenile diabetes], due to damage of β - cells of\n  islets of Langerhans → low level of plasma insulin.\n  • Characterized by polyphagia, polydipsia, and polyuria.\n  • Controlled by insulin & diet.\n  Type II Diabetes mellitus (non-insulin-dependent) insulin resistant:\n  • In overweight individuals older than 40 years.\n  • It is due to decreased binding of insulin to its receptors,\n  • Usually controlled by diet.\n 38  HISTOLOGY - Dr AHMED ZAHRA\n  Chapter\n  GIT\n  C. Liver\n The most important and largest intra-abdominal organ.\n It is a compound tubular gland & mixed gland, as it has:\n  1) exocrine secretion (bile),\n  2) endocrine secretion: substances that pass directly to the blood, e.g. glucose,"}],"Renal corpuscle (podocytes, filtration barrier)":[{"src":"Zahra histology notes","head":"• E.M. Picture of podocytes:","text":" Have a cell body from which arise several primary (major) processes.\n   1ry processes give rise to secondary (minor) processes or pedicels, that\n  embrace a portion of glomerular capillary.\n   Minor processes of adjacent podocytes interdigitate with gaps inbetween.\n   These gaps are called filtration slits, which are coverd by a thin\n  semipermeable diaphragm named filtration slit diaphragm.\n   Podocyte cell body is separated from glomerular basement membrane by\n  subpodocytic space.\n   Cytoplasm of podocytes contains : numerous free ribosomes, mitochondria,\n  rER, and prominent Golgi complex. Bundles of actin filaments are present in\n  cytoplasm and process that give podocytes a contractile capacity.\n  • EM of filtration slit diaphragm\n   zipper-like sheet is formed by transmembrane protein nephrin, has a\n  central dense bar with pores on both sides.\n  • Functions of Podocyte:\n   Involved in formation of the blood renal barrier.\n   Renewal of the basement membrane of glomerular capillaries.\n  HISTOLOGY - Dr AHMED ZAHRA  51\n Chapter\n  URINARY SYSTEM\n52  HISTOLOGY - Dr AHMED ZAHRA\n  Chapter\n  URINARY SYSTEM\n Blood Renal Barrier (Glomerular Filtration Barrier) (GFB)\n  • Definition: it is the barrier that separates blood inside glomerular capillaries\n  from the glomerular filtrate inside Bowman's space and through which\n  filtration of blood occurs.\n  • Structure: formed of 3 layers :\n  1) Fenestrated endothelial cells of glomerular capillary:"},{"src":"Zahra histology notes","head":"❶ Cortical Nephron: (present in cortex) has short loop of Henle.","text":"❷ Juxta medullary Nephron: (present close to medulla), has long loop of Henle.\n  ❸ Intermediate (midcortical) Nephron: (present in the midregion of the cortex),\n  has a loop of Henle of intermediate length.\n Histological structure of nephron: Each nephron consists of:\n  1- Renal (Malpighian) Corpuscle.\n  2- Proximal convoluted tubule (PCT).\n  3- Loop of Henle.\n  4- Distal convoluted tubule (DCT).\n  HISTOLOGY - Dr AHMED ZAHRA  49\n  Chapter\n  URINARY SYSTEM\n  1- Renal (Malpighian) Corpuscle\nA vascular ball (200 μm), formed by invagination of spherical blind end of nephron by a\ntuft of capillaries (glomerulus) changing it into a double- walled cup (Bowman's capsule).\n Renal corpuscle has 2 poles:\n  ❶ Vascular pole: where afferent arteriole enters, and efferent arteriole leaves the\n  corpuscle.\n  - Afferent arteriole : carrying unfiltered blood, divides and subdivides forming\n  a tuft of capillaries that drains into the efferent arteriole\n  - Efferent arteriole : which carries filtered blood outside the nephron.\n  - Afferent arteriole has a thicker media and a larger diameter than efferent\n  arteriole (to creates a high glomerular pressure to produce large quantities of\n  glomerular filtrate).\n  ❷ Urinary pole: Where renal corpuscle becomes continuous with PCT.\n Renal corpuscle consists of: Glomerulus of blood capillaries and Bowman's Capsule.\n  ❶ Renal Glomerulus\n  - LM: Lined by simple squamous endothelium, surrounded by a thick,\n  continuous basement membrane (PAS positive).\n  - EM:"},{"src":"Zahra histology notes","head":" Endothelium is fenestrated with open pores (Pores are NOT covered by","text":"diaphragms).\n   The Glomerular Basement Membrane (GBM) is formed 3 layers:\n  (1) Lamina rara (lucida) externa: an outer electron-lucent layer.\n  (2) Lamina rara (lucida) interna: an inner electron-lucent layer.\n  (3) Lamina densa: electron-dense central layer, lies between laminae rarae\n50  HISTOLOGY - Dr AHMED ZAHRA\n  Chapter\n  URINARY SYSTEM\n❷ Bowman's Capsule\n  - Formed of two continuous layers:\n  1) Outer parietal layer:\n  lined by simple\n  squamous epithelium\n  continuous with\n  the simple cuboidal\n  epithelium of the\n  proximal convoluted\n  tubule.\n  2) Inner visceral layer:\n  formed of modified\n  simple squamous\n  epithelial cells called\n  podocytes. It directly\n  invests the glomerular\n  capillaries.\n  - Parietal and visceral layers are separated by \"Capsular Space\" or \"Bowman's\n  Space\", which receives glomerular filtrate.\n Podocytes:\n  • Modified simple squamous epithelium of visceral layer of Bowman's capsule."}],"Mesangial cells and blood-renal barrier":[{"src":"Zahra histology notes","head":"Filtration selectivity depends on","text":"a) Molecular weight (M.W.) & size. It allows passage of molecules smaller\n  than blood cells and platelets e.g. plasma proteins.\n  2) Glomerular basement membrane (GBM): The only continuous and\n  the most effective layer of GFB, is formed by fusion of basal laminae of\n  glomerular endothelium and podocyte.\n  Filtration selectivity depends on:\n  a) Molecular weight: it prevents substances with large molecular weight\n  (160,000 Daltons) as plasma proteins, but small molecules as simple\n  sugars and amino acids can pass through it.\n  b) Molecular Charges: Both laminae rarae are negatively charged, so,\n  impedes the passage of all negatively charged molecules eg. Albumin\n  (although its M.W. is 69,000 Daltons).\n  3) Filtration slit diaphragms: Diaphragms that cover the slits (gaps) between\n  podocyte minor processes (pedicels).\n  Filtration selectivity depends on:\n  a) Molecular weight: allow the passage of low M.W. substances (less than\n  40,000 Daltons).\n  b) Molecular charges: As it is negatively charged, it impedes the passage of\n  negatively charged molecules\n  HISTOLOGY - Dr AHMED ZAHRA  53\n Chapter\n  URINARY SYSTEM\n  Mesangial Cells:\n  • LM & EM:\n   Stellate shaped cells with multiple processes.\n   small darkly stained nuclei and basophilic cytoplasm rich in lysosomes.\n  • Types & Sites: There are two groups of mesangial cells:\n   Extraglomerular (LAcis cells) located at the vascular pole.\n   Intraglomerular, located in renal corpuscle, inbetween glomerular capillaries."}],"PCT vs DCT":[{"src":"Zahra histology notes","head":"• Functions of Mesangial Cells:","text":"1) Supportive function and renewal of GBM: by secretion of collagen,\n  chondroitin sulphate, fibronectin and mesangial matrix.\n  2) Contractile function: Involved in regulating blood flow within the\n  glomerular capillaries by responding to vasoactive substances.\n  3) Phagocytic function: They can extend their processes between the\n  endothelial lining of glomerular capillaries to phagocytose any foreign\n  debris, or residues during renewal of GBM.\n  4) Extraglomerular mesangial cells form part of juxtaglomerular apparatus.\n  Differences between proximal and distal convoluted tubules\n  2- Proximal convoluted tubule  3- Distal convoluted tubule\n  (PCT)  (DCT)\n  - More convoluted.  - Less convoluted.\n  - Longer (15 mm).  - Shorter (5 mm).\nShape & Size\n  - Larger diameter (60 µm).  - Smaller (40 µm).\n  - Very narrow lumen.  - Wider lumen\n54  HISTOLOGY - Dr AHMED ZAHRA\n  Chapter\n  URINARY SYSTEM\n  - end of ascending limb of loop\n  Site: Starts  - Urinary pole of renal corpuscle.\n  of Henle.\n  - The beginning of descending\n  Ends  - Ends by collecting tubule.\n  limb of loop of Henle.\n  - 3-5 cells in cross section.  - 5-8, up to 20 in macula densa.\n  - High cuboidal (pyramidal).  - Cubical cells (wide lumen).\n  - III-defined cell boundaries.  - Clear cell boundaries.\n  L.M. of Lining"}],"Collecting tubules (principal and intercalated cells)":[{"src":"Zahra histology notes","head":"urine conc.)","text":"• cortical nephrons have short loops.\n• It is formed of:\n  1- Thick descending limb: similar to PCT lined with high cuboidal epithelium.\n  2- Thin descending limb: lined with simple squamous epithelium.\n  3- Thin ascending limb: lined with simple squamous epithelium.\n  4- Thick ascending limb: similar to DCT, lined with simple cubical epithelium.\n• Function: making urine hypertonic and conserving water.\n Connecting Tubules:\n  - The distal convoluted tubules of several nephrons join to form a short\n  connecting tubule that leads to the collecting duct.\n  - Epithelial lining undergoes a gradual transition from DCT to collecting duct.\n  - Play an important role in K+ secretion which is regulated by mineralo-corticoids\n  secreted by adrenal cortex\n  B) Collecting tubules and ducts:\n• The excretory portion of uriniferous tubules.\n• Collecting tubules are about 20 mm long and have 3 recognized regions: cortical,\n  medullary and papillary.\n  ❶ Cortical collecting tubules:\n   Present in cortical medullary rays.\n   Lined by light principal cells and dark intercalated cells.\n  ❷ Medullary collecting tubules:\n   Formed by the union of several cortical collecting tubules.\n   Larger diameter than corical collecting tubules.\n   Lined by light principal cells and dark intercalated cells,\n   tubules in the inner zone of medulla that have principal cells only.\n  ❸ Papillary collecting tubules (ducts of Bellini):\n   Each 6-8 of the medullary collecting tubules open into a wide straight collecting\n  duct (duct of Bellini).\n   As they penetrate deeper into medulla, their lining epithelial cells increase in"},{"src":"Zahra histology notes","head":"• in states of alkanemia type B intercalated cells secrete excess HCO3 in urine.","text":"Read Only\n  • Kidney stones are formed in renal pelvis or calyces. When they move from\n  renal pelvis to ureters →obstruction of ureters or severe pain. They can be\n  removed surgically or ultrasonic shock.\n  • Diabetes Insipidus insufficient production of ADH. There is inadequate\n  reabsorption of water from the collecting tubules→ excessive production of\n  diluted hypotonic pale urine. But Blood sugar level is normal.\n  HISTOLOGY - Dr AHMED ZAHRA  57\n  Chapter\n  URINARY SYSTEM\n Juxtaglomerular (JG) Apparatus\n • Site : It is Located at the vascular pole of the renal corpuscle\n • Structure :\n  ❶ Macula Densa: It is the part of the DCT that fits between the afferent and\n  efferent arterioles at the vascular pole of the renal corpuscle.\n   Cells of macula densa show the following changes:\n  - The length of cells increases, become columnar.\n  - The nuclei of cells become apical, deeply-stained and closely packed, thus\n  appear as a \"dark spot\" = macula, by L.M.\n  - Golgi complex is situated infra-nuclear (basal).\n  - Basement membrane is lost, so these cells come in contact with JG cells.\n   Function: act as osmoreceptors that monitor level of Na ions in lumen of DCT\n  ❷ Juxtaglomerular cells (JG cells):\n   Modified, smooth muscle cells present in the tunica media of afferent\n  arteriole (and sometimes the efferent arteriole).\n   The JG Cells show the following changes:\n  a) Nuclei become rounded instead of being elongated.\n  b) Cytoplasm contains secretory granules (contain Renin) that stains with PAS.\n   Function: Secrete Renin enzyme."},{"src":"Zahra histology notes","head":"height till they become columnar.","text":" Ducts of Bellini open into the tips of medullary pyramids (renal papilla).\n   They are the major factor in urine concentrating mechanism under the effect\n  of ADH.\n56  HISTOLOGY - Dr AHMED ZAHRA\n  Chapter\n  URINARY SYSTEM\n  Differences between principal and intercalated cells\n  Principal cells  Intercalated cells\n  Number  Numerous  Few\n  LM  Pale  dark\n  Types  One type  Alpha & beta types\n  Organelles  Few  Numerous\n  Apical Microvilli  Single cilium & scanty, short.  Many microvilli\n  Apical endocytosis  Few  Many\n E/M:\n  cell boundaries  clear  ill-defined\n  Mitochondria  Few, small & spherical  More\n  Basal infoldings  Numerous (ion transport)  Absent\n  Increase water permeability of\n  Regulation of acid\n  collecting ducts as they are very\n  base balance: by\n  Functions  sensitive to ADH, due to presence\n  secreting H⁺.by acells\n  of Aquaporins (a specific trans-\n  & HCO3 by b- cells.\n  membrane protein) water channels\n  }  • Under states of acidemia, kidney uses type A intercalated cells to secrete\n  excess H+ in urine and generate more HCO3."}],"Juxtaglomerular apparatus and macula densa":[{"src":"Zahra histology notes","head":"❸ Extraglomerular mesangial cells (Lacis cells, pole cushions):","text":" Small, pale stained cells that occupy the space bounded by the afferent\n  arteriole, efferent arteriole and macula densa.\n   Function: Supportive and may transmit signals from macula densa into\n  glomerulus, affecting vasoconstriction there.\n • Functions of Juxtaglomerular Apparatus: regulation of glomerular filtration\n  rate & blood pressure through \"The renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system\" as\n  follows:\n  1. As a result of drop in blood pressure or blood volume (as in hemorrhage) →\n  glomerular filtrate volume decreases.\n  2. Na+ and Cl- ions concentration in the DCT decreases.\n  3. Macula densa monitors these changes, stimulates JG cells to secrete renin.\n  4. Renin changes angiotensinogen (present in bloodstream & formed in the liver)\n  → angiotensin I, then into angiotensin II (by angiotensin converting enzyme\n  (ACE) present in lung capillaries.\n  5. Angiotensin II, a potent vasoconstrictor, raises blood pressure by:\n  i) Constriction of peripheral blood vessels, and\n  ii) Release of aldosterone from adrenal cortex and ADH from pituitary gland.\n  6. Aldosterone promotes reabsorption of NaCl by DCT. ADH → reabsorption of\n  water from collecting tubules → increase blood volume & blood pressure.\n  N.B.: The return of normal blood pressure inhibits renin secretion by JG cells.\n58  HISTOLOGY - Dr AHMED ZAHRA\n  Chapter\nURINARY SYSTEM\n  HISTOLOGY - Dr AHMED ZAHRA  59\n  Chapter\n  URINARY SYSTEM\n  BLOOD SUPPLY OF THE KIDNEY\n Renal artery: enters the hilum, giving rise to\n  interlobar arteries that extend between the"}]};
